\id JOB Original Douay-Rheims (1582/1610)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Job
\toc1 The Book of Job.
\toc2 Job
\toc3 JOB
\mt1 The Book of Job.
\is THE ARGUMENT OF THE BOOKE OF IOB.
\ip \it Holie Iob\it*, otherwiſe called \it Iobab\it* (Gen. 36.) as S. Auguſtin, S. Chriſoſtom, S. Ambroſe, S. \f - \ft \+it Li. 18. c. 47. ciuit. conc. 2. de Laza. Rom. 9. Præfat. in Iob.\+it*\f* Gregorie, and other fathers teach, \it the ſonne of Zara, the ſonne of Rahuel, the ſonne of Eſau\it*, was King \f - \ft Iob of the race of Eſau.\f* (or abſolute Prince) of the land of Hus. Who being perfect in religion, ſincere in life, rich in wealth, and bleſſed with children, for an admirable example of patience, and to shew that a mortal \f - \ft VVhy God ſuffered him to be ſo afflicted. The contentes according to the hiſtorie.\f* man through Gods grace, may reſiſt al the devil's tentations, by Gods permiſſion, ſudainly loſt al his goodes and children, was ſtriken with horrible ſores in al his bodie, reuiled by his wife, and inſtead of comfort which his ſpecial freindes pretended towards him, was iniuriouſly charged by them, with impatience, arrogancie, blaſphemie, and other crimes, for which they falſly ſuppoſed he was ſo afflicted, affirming, and by diuers ſophiſtical argumentes, grounded as they pretended vpon Gods iuſtice, wiſdome, powre, mercie, and goodnes, would proue that God ſuffereth none but wicked men to be ſo afflicted. But Iob conſtantly defendeth his owne iuſt innocencie, and that worldlie calamities, and proſperitie happen indifferently to good and bad in this life, and that the true reward of the iuſt, & punishment of the wicked, is to be expected in the other world. At laſt God, with due reprehenſion of Iob for ſome imperfections, sharply rebuketh the errors and inſolencie of his aduerſe freindes, giveth ſentence on Iobs ſide, pardoneth them at his interceſſion, and reſtoreth al thinges to him duble to that he had before.
\ip Beſides the literal ſenſe, \it Iob\it* in al his actions, ſufferinges, and whole life, was \it a ſpecial figure of Chriſt, shewing\it* (ſayth S. Gregorie) \it by thoſe thinges which he did and ſuſteyned, what our\it* \f - \ft Iob an eſpecial figure of Chriſt.\f* \f - \ft \+it Præfat.\+it*\f* \it Redemer should do and ſuffer\it* yea more particularly then moſt part of the Patriarches, which \it S. Ierome\it* (epiſt. ad Paulin.) alſo admireth and teſtifieth, ſaying: \it what myſteries\it* of Chriſt \it doth not this booke comprehend? Euerie word is ful of ſenſe.\it* Moreouer this hiſtorie is replenished with \it moral documents\it*, how to embrace vertue, and eſchew vice: propoſing the life of a right godlie man, \f - \ft Moral documentes in this booke.\f* neither inſolent in proſperitie, not deſparing in aduerſitie, alwayes reſolute in Gods ſeruice, as Wel in his proſperous kingdom as in the miſerable dunghil. Here alſo we haue the true maner of arguing, according to the rules of Logike, with detection of ſophiſtrie, \it Iob prouing and\it* \f - \ft True logike & Philoſophie.\f* \it diſprouing aſſertions by propoſition, aſſumption, and concluſion\it*, as S. Ierom obſerueth, with profound knowlege of natural thinges and cawſes, and appeareth in very manie place. Al which varietie and abundance of matter, compriſed in ſmal room, make manie thinges hard and obſcure, yet are the ſame ſo tempered with other thinges plaine and eaſie, that here is verified S. \f - \ft Hard and eaſie thinges to be vnderſtood are both profitable.\f* Auguſtins obſeruation (li. 2. c. 6. doct. Chriſt) \it certaine places of holie Scriptures ſerue as delectable meate to them that hunger and thirſt diuine knowlege, and the obſcure take away tediouſnes from them, that loath vſual plaine doctrin\it*.
\ip It is moſt probable that Iob himſelf, inſpired by the Holie Ghoſt, by whoſe grace he excelled al in \f - \ft VVritten by Iob himſelf moſt part in verſe.\f* right ſimplicitie (c. 1.) writte his owne hiſtorie; the moſt part in verſe, only the two firſt chapters and the laſt in proſe, in the Arabian tongue; which Moyſes tranſlated into Hebrew, for the conſolation of Iſraelites afflicted in Ægypt.
\ip And it may be diuided into three general partes. Firſt the change of Iobs ſtate from proſperitie into \f - \ft Diuided into three partes.\f* affliction, with his lamentation for the ſame, are recorded in the three firſt chapters. In foure and thirtie chapters folowing are ſundrie diſputations, conflictes, and diſcourſes betwen him and his freindes, touching the cauſe of his ſo vehement affliction. In the fiue laſt chapters God diſcuſſeth the quarel, giveth ſentence for Iob againſt his aduerſaries, pardoneth them, and rewardeth him.
\c 1
\cd Holie Iob offereth ſacrifice for euerie one of his children, 6. whoſe good eſtate Satan enuying, by Gods permiſsion ſpoyleth him of al his goodes, and children, 20. for which he being penſiue offendeth not, but thanketh God for al. \f - \ft This book is read the two firſt weeks of September.\f*
\p
\v 1 THERE \f 1 \fr 1.1 \ft The firſt part. The change of Job's proſperous ſtate into affliction.\f* was a man in the Land of Hus, named Iob, & that man was ſimple, & right, and fearing God, and departing from euil.
\v 2 And there were borne to him ſeuen ſonnes and three daughters.
\v 3 And his poſſeſſion was ſeuen thouſand sheepe, and three thouſand camels, alſo fiue hundred yoke of oxen and fiue hundred she aſſes, and a familie exceding great: and that man was great among al them of the Eaſt.
\v 4 And his ſonnes went, and made a feaſt by houſes, euerie one in his day. And ſending they called their three ſiſters, to eate and drinke with them.
\v 5 And when the dayes of feaſting had paſſed about in courſe, Iob ſent to them, and ſanctified them: and riſing vp early offered holocauſtes for euerie one. For he ſayd: Leſt perhaps my ſonnes haue ſinned, and haue bleſſed God in their hartes. So did Iob al the dayes.
\v 6 But on a certaine day when the ſonnes of God were come to aſſiſt before our Lord, \f a \fr 1.6 \ft Diuels appeare not in Gods ſight, but ſometime in preſence of Angels which repreſent God \+it S. Athan. q. 8. ad Antiochum\+it*.\f* Satan alſo was preſent amongſt them.
\v 7 To whom our Lord ſayd: From whence comeſt thou? Who anſwering, ſayd: I haue gone round about the earth, and walked through it.
\v 8 And our Lord ſayd to him: Haſt thou conſidered my ſeruant Iob, that there is not the like to him in the earth, a man ſimple, and right and fearing God, and departing from euil.?
\v 9 To whom Satan anſwering, ſaid: Why, doth Iob feare God in vayne?
\v 10 haſt not thou fenſed him, and his houſe, and al his ſubſtance round about, bleſſed the workes of his handes, and his poſſeſſion hath increaſed on the earth?
\v 11 But ſtretch forth thy hand a little, and touch al thinges that he poſſeſſeth, vnleſſe he bleſſe thee in the face.
\v 12 Our Lord therfore ſayd to Satan: \f b \fr 1.12 \ft God doth not directly ſend euils but permitteth them to happen to his ſeruantes.\f* behold, al things that he hath, are in thy hand, onlie vpon him extend not thy hand. And Satan went forth from the face of our Lord.
\v 13 And when vpon a certeine day his ſonnes and daughters did eate, and drinke wyne in the houſe of their eldeſt brother,
\v 14 there came a meſſenger to Iob, which ſayd: The oxen plowghed, and the she aſſes fed beſide them,
\v 15 and the Sabeians came in violently, & haue taken al things, and haue ſtroken the ſeruantes with the ſword, and I onlie haue eſcaped to tel thee.
\v 16 And when he yet ſpake, an other came, and ſayd: The fire of God fel from heauen, and ſtriking the sheepe and the ſeruantes hath conſumed them, and I only haue eſcaped to tel thee.
\v 17 But whiles he alſo was yet ſpeaking, there came an other and ſaid: The Chaldees made three troupes, and haue inuaded the camels, and taken them, moreouer the ſeruantes alſo they haue ſtrooken with the ſword, and I alone am fled to tel thee.
\v 18 He yet ſpake, and behold an other came in, and ſaid: Thy ſonnes and daughters eating and drinking wine in the houſe of their eldeſt brother,
\v 19 ſodenly a vehement winde came violently from the countrie of the deſert, and shooke the foure corners of the houſe, which falling oppreſſed thy children and they are dead, and I alone haue eſcaped to tel thee.
\v 20 Then Iob roſe vp, and rent his garmentes, and with powled head falling on the ground, adored,
\v 21 and ſaid \f c \fr 1.21 \ft By this holie example ancient fathers condeme the ſenſles Stoikes who are neuer moued, S. Aug li. 1. c. 9. ciuit. S. Paul alſo reproueth men without affection. Rom. 1. v. 31.\f* Naked came I out of my mothers wombe, and naked shal I returne thither: Our Lord gaue, and our Lord hath taken away: as it hath pleaſed our Lord, ſo is it done: the name of our Lord be bleſſed.
\v 22 In al theſe thinges Iob ſinned not with his lippes, neither ſpake he anie foolish thing againſt God.
\c 2
\cd Satan by Gods permiſſion, 7. ſtriketh Iob with ſores from the ſole of his foote to the toppe of his head. 9. His wife alſo inſulteth againſt him, but he ſinneth not. 11 Three freindes coming to viſite and conforte him, ſitte ſilent by him ſeuen dayes.
\p
\v 1 AND it came to paſſe when on a certaine day the ſonnes of God were come, and ſtood before our Lord, and Satan came among them, and ſtood in his ſight,
\v 2 that our Lord ſayd to Satan: From whence comeſt thou? who anſwering ſayd: I haue gone round about the earth, and walked through it.
\v 3 And our Lord ſayd to Satan: Haſt thou conſidered my ſeruant Iob, that there is not the like to him in the earth, a man \f a \fr 2.3 \ft A man of plaine and true dealing mixed with meeknes without al guile is called a ſimple right man \+it S. Greg. li. 1. Moral. c. 2 & ſeq\+it*.\f* ſimple, and right, and fearing God, and departing from euil, and yet reteyning innocencie? But thou haſt moued me againſt him, that I should afflict him in vaine.
\v 4 To whom Satan anſwering, ſaid: Skinne for skinne, & al thinges that a man hath, he wil geue for his life:
\v 5 otherwiſe put thy hand, and touch his bone and flesh, and then shalt thou ſee that he wil bleſſe thee in the face.
\v 6 Our Lord therfore ſayd to Satan: Behold he is in thy hand, but yet ſaue his life.
\v 7 Satan therfore going out from the face of our Lord, ſtroke Iob with a verie ſore boile, from the ſole of the foote euen to the toppe of his head:
\v 8 who with a shel ſcraped the corruption, ſitting on a dunghil.
\v 9 And \f b \fr 2.9 \ft His wife perſwading him to deſperation and blaſphemie, ſignified carnal cogitions which corrupt the ſoule inwardly, as afflictions do aſſault the flesh outwardly \+it S. Greg. li. 3. c. 24\+it*.\f* his wife ſayd to, him: Doeſt thou yet continue in thy ſimplicitie? bleſſe God and die.
\v 10 Who ſayd to her: Thou haſt ſpoken like one of the foolish wemen, if we haue receiued good things of the hand of God, euil thinges why should we not receiue? In al theſe things Iob ſinned not with his lippes.
\v 11 Therfore Iobs three frendes hearing al the euil, that had changed to him, came euerie one out of their place, Elephaz a Themanite, and Baldad a Suhite, and Sophar a Naamathite. For they had appointed, that coming togeather they would viſite him, and confort him.
\v 12 And when they had lifted vp their eies a far of, they knew him not, and crying out they wept, and renting their clothes ſprinkled. duſt ouer their head toward heauen.
\v 13 And they ſate wirh him on the ground \f c \fr 2.13 \ft Seuen dayes together euerie day and night ſome good part.\f* ſeuen daies and ſeuen nightes, and no man ſpake to him a word: for they ſaw the payne to be vehement.
\c 3
\cd Iob lamenteth, deſcribing his owne, and the general calamities of man, 13. and shewing howe eſcape manie miſeries which either are neſter borne or dye preſently after their borth.
\p
\v 1 AFTER theſe things Iob opened his mouth, and \f a \fr 3.1 \ft After ſo long ſilence at laſt Iob expreſſeth his grief before his freindes to be ſo great, that in reſpect therof, he iuſtly curſeth al euil thinges as not made by God but adioyned to mans life, being the effectes of ſinne, euen from his conception and birth: wishing that whatſoeuer concurred to his longer afflictions in this life, and hindered his more ſpedie deliuerie from dangers and calamities had not bene, for ſo he had benne ſowner at reſt as Gods goodnes should diſpoſe of him Al which was a lawful deſire and no ſinne at al \+it See I. Pineda. in c. 2. Iob\+it*.\f* curſed his day,
\v 2 and ſpake.
\v 3 Perish may the day wherin I was borne, and the night wherin it was ſayd: A man is conceiued.
\v 4 Be that day turned into darkeneſſe, God require it not from aboue, and let it not be lightened with-light.
\v 5 Let darkenes, and the shadow of death obſcure it, let a miſt poſſeſſe it, and be it wrapped in bitterneſſe.
\v 6 A darkeſome hurle wind poſſeſſe that night, be it not counted in the daies of the yeare, nor numbred in the monethes.
\v 7 Be that night ſolitarie, nor prayſe worthie.
\v 8 Let them curſe it which curſe the day, which are readie to raiſe vp Leuiathan.
\v 9 Let the ſtarres be darkened with the miſt therof: let it expect light and let it not ſee, neither the ryſing of the appearing morning.
\v 10 Becauſe it shut not vp the doores of the wombe, that bare me, nor tooke away euils from myne eies.
\v 11 Why died I not in the matrice, periſhed not forthwith being come forth of the wombe?
\v 12 Why receiued vpon the knees? why nurced with the breaſtes?
\v 13 For now ſleping I should be quiet, and should reſt in my ſleepe:
\v 14 With kinges and conſules of the earth, which build themſelues ſolitarie places:
\v 15 Or with princes, that poſſeſſe gold, and replenish their houſes with ſiluer:
\v 16 Or as a thing vntimely borne that is hid I should not be, or as they that being conceiued haue not ſeene the light.
\v 17 There the impious haue ceaſed from tumult, & there the wearied with ſtrength haue reſted.
\v 18 And they ſometime bound together without griefe, haue not heard the voyce of the exactor.
\v 19 Litle and great are there, and the ſeruant free from his maſter.
\v 20 Why is there light geuen to a miſerable man, and life to them, that are in bitterneſſe of ſoule?
\v 21 That expect death, and it cometh not, as they that dig vp treaſure.
\v 22 And they reioyce excedingly when they haue found the graue.
\v 23 To a man whoſe life is hid, and God hath compaſſed him with darknes?
\v 24 Before I eate I ſigh: and as it were ouerflowing waters to my roaring:
\v 25 Becauſe the feare which I feared, hath chanced to me: and that which I was afrayd of, hath happened.
\v 26 haue I not diſſembled? haue I not kept ſilence? haue I not beene at eaſe? and indignation is come vpon me.
\c 4
\cd Eliphaz blameth Iob as guiltie of impatience, arguing thereupon that he was not ſo perfect in vertue as he ſemed. 7 and therfore is now punished by God, who (as Ehiphaz falſly ſuppoſeth) afflicteth not innocent men. 12. alleaging for proofe an imaginarie viſion. \f - \ft The ſecond part. Diverſe diſcourſes & diſputes about the cauſe of Job'ſ afflictions.\f*
\p
\v 1 BVT \f 1 \fr 4.1 \ft The firſt conflict, betwen Eliphaz and Iob.\f* Eliphaz the Themanite anſwering, ſayd:
\v 2 If we shal begin to ſpeake to thee, perhaps thou wilt take it greuouſly, but the word conceiued who can hold?
\v 3 Behold thou haſt taught manie, & wearie handes thou haſt ſtrengthned:
\v 4 Them that wauered thy wordes haue confirmed, and trembling knees thou haſt ſtrengthened:
\v 5 But now a plague is come vpon thee, and thou haſt faynred: hath touched thee, and art trubled.
\v 6 Where is thy feare, thy ſtrength, thy patience, and the perfection of thy wayes?
\v 7 Remember I beſech thee, who euer being innocent hath \f a \fr 4.7 \ft No innocent euer periſhed eternally: but innocent Abel was ſlaine temporaly, and innumerable others ſuffer calamities for their greater merite.\f* perished? or when haue the iuſt bene deſtroyed?
\v 8 Yea rather I haue ſene them, that worke iniquitie, and ſow ſorrowes, & reape them,
\v 9 to haue perished by the blaſt of God, and with the ſpirit of his wrath to haue bene conſumed.
\v 10 The roaring of the lion, and the voice of the lioneſſe, & the teeth of the whelpes of lions are bruiſed:
\v 11 The tigre hath perished, becauſe he had no praye, and the lions whelpes are deſtroyed.
\v 12 More-ouer \f b \fr 4.12 \ft Heretikes pretend ſuch obſcure viſions more to gette credite then to edifie others. \+it S. Greg. li. 5. c. 18\+it*.\f* to me there was ſpoken a ſecret word, and as it were by ſtealth hath mine eare receiued the vaines of the whiſpering therof.
\v 13 In the horrour of a viſion by night, when deepe ſleepe is wont to hold men,
\v 14 feare held me, and trembling, and al my bones were made ſore afrayd:
\v 15 And when the ſpirit paſſed in my preſence, the heares of my flesh ſtood vpright.
\v 16 There ſtood one, whoſe countenance I knew not, an image before mine eies, and I heard the voyce as it were of a gentle winde.
\v 17 What, \f c \fr 4.17 \ft Iob eaſely granteth that man may not compare nor contend with God \+it ch. 9. v. 1\+it*. Yet men may be innocent & free from greuous ſinnes.\f* shal man be iuſtified in compariſon of God, or shal a man be more pure then his maker?
\v 18 Behold they that ſerue him, are not ſtable, and in his Angels he found wickednes:
\v 19 How much more they that inhabite houſes of clay, which haue an earthly foundation, shal be conſumed as it were of the moth?
\v 20 From morning vntil euening they shal be cut downe: and becauſe none vnderſtandeth, they shal perish foreuer.
\v 21 And they that shal be leaſt, shal be taken away from them: they shal die, and not in wiſedom.
\c 5
\cd Eliphaz proſecuteth his diſcourſe to conuince Iob of great ſinnes, because he is ſo vehemently afflicted. 17. exhorteth him therfore to acknowlege his ſinnes, ſo al thinges shal ſuccede proſperouſly.
\p
\v 1 CAL therfore \f a \fr 5.1 \ft This diſputer hauing pretended an imaginarie viſion from God againſt Iob, now he ſuppoſeth that neither God nor Angel nor other holie perſon wil patronage his cauſe, nor iudge of his caſe as he doth, but that al wil condemne him of impatience, follie, enuie, and other ſinnes.\f* if there be that wil anſwer thee, & turne to ſome of the ſainctes.
\v 2 Anger in deede killeth the foolish, and enuie ſleaeth the litle one.
\v 3 I haue ſeene a foole with firme roote, and I curſed his beautie by and by.
\v 4 His children shalbe made far from ſaluation, and shal be deſtroyed in the gate, and there shalbe none to deliuer.
\v 5 Whoſe harueſt the hungrie shal eate, & the armed shal take him by violence, and the thirſtie ſhal drinke his riches.
\v 6 Nothing in the earth is done without a cauſe, and out of the ground ſorrow shal not riſe.
\v 7 \f b \fr 5.7 \ft This prouerb importeth that a man muſt not thincke to paſſe his life without trauel, but muſt \+it get his bread with ſvveat of his brovves\+it*, or ſuffer other calamities.\f* Man is borne to labour, and the bird to flight.
\v 8 For the which thing I wil beſech our Lord, and toward God I wil ſet my ſpeach:
\v 9 Who doeth great and vnſearchable and meruelous things without number:
\v 10 Who geueth raine vpon the face of the earth, and watereth al thinges with waters:
\v 11 Who ſetteth the humble on high, and them that are in heauineſſe he conforteth with health:
\v 12 Who diſſipateth the cogitations of the malignant, that their handes can not accomplish that which they began:
\v 13 Who apprehendeth the wiſe in their ſubteltie, and diſſipateth the counſel of the wicked:
\v 14 By day they shal incurre darkeneſſe, and as it were in the night, ſo shal they grope at noone daies.
\v 15 Moreouer he shal ſaue the needy from the ſword of their mouth, and the poore from the hand of the violent.
\v 16 And to the needie there shal be hope, but iniquitie shal draw together her mouth.
\v 17 Bleſſed is the man that is corrected of God: refuſe not therfore the chaſtiſing of our Lord:
\v 18 Becauſe he woundeth, and cureth: ſtriketh, and his hands shal heale.
\v 19 In \f c \fr 5.19 \ft Gods goodnes deliuereth his ſeruantes the ſpace of this laborious life,\f* ſix tribulations he shal deliuer thee, and in the \f d \fr 5.19 \ft And moſt eſpecially in the houre of death. \+it S. Grg. li. 6, c. 18\+it*.\f* ſeuenth euil shal not touch thee.
\v 20 In famine he shal deliuer thee from death; and in battel, from the hand of the ſword.
\v 21 From the ſcourge of the tongue thou shalt be hid; & thou shalt not feare calamitie when it cometh.
\v 22 In waſte and famine thou shalt laugh; and the beaſtes of the earth thou shalt not feare.
\v 23 But with the ſtones of the landes thy couenant, and the beaſtes of the earth shal be peaceable to thee.
\v 24 And thou shalt know that thy tabernacle hath peace, and viſiting thy beautie, thou shalt not ſinne.
\v 25 Thou shalt know alſo that thy ſeed shal be manifold, and thy progenie as the graſſe of the earth.
\v 26 Thou shalt enter into the graue in abundance, as a heape of wheate is caryed in his time.
\v 27 Behold, this is euen ſo, as we haue ſearched out: which thou hauing heard reuolue in thy mind.
\c 6
\cd Iob anſwereth the obiections of Eliphaz, shewing that in deede the calamitie which he ſuffereth is much greatter than his ſinnes deſerue; and therfore his lamentation is excuſable. 8. wisheth (if it ſo pleaſe God) that he may dye. 13. complaneth that his freindes are become his aduerſeries, 16. grauely expoſtulateth that they reprehend him, 22. and helpe him not.
\p
\v 1 BVT Iob anſwering, ſayd:
\v 2 \f a \fr 6.2 \ft A man of ſincere conſcience confeſſeth the ſinnes wherof he is guiltie, yet acknowledgeth not al wherwith others may vniuſtly charge him.\f* Would God my ſinnes were weyed, wherby I haue deſerued wrath, and the calamitie, which I ſuffer in a balance.
\v 3 As the ſand of the ſea this would appeare heauier, wherfore my wordes alſo are ful of ſorrow:
\v 4 Becauſe the arrowes of our Lord be in me, the indignation whereof drinketh vp my ſpirit, and the terrours of our Lord warre agaynſt me.
\v 5 Wil the wilde aſſe roare when he hath graſſe? or the oxe loweth when he shal ſtand before the ful manger.
\v 6 Or can an vnſauerie thing be eaten, that is not ſeaſoned with ſalt? or can a man taſt that which being taſted bringeth death?
\v 7 The thinges which before my ſoule would not touch, now for anguish are my meates.
\v 8 Who wil graunt that my petition may come: and that God would geue me that which I expect?
\v 9 And he that hath begune, the ſame would looſe his hand, and cut me of?
\v 10 And this might be my comfort, that afflicting me with ſorrow, he ſpare not, nor gayneſay the wordes of the Holie one.
\v 11 For What is my ſtrength, that I can ſuſteyne it? or what is mine end, that I should doe patiently.
\v 12 Neither is my ſtrength the ſtrength of ſtones, neither is my flesh of braſſe.
\v 13 Behold there is no help for me in my ſelf, and my familiar freindes alſo are departed from me.
\v 14 He that taketh away mercie from his frend, forſaketh the feare of our Lord.
\v 15 My brethren haue paſſed by me, as the torrent that paſſeth ſwiftly in the valleys.
\v 16 They that feare the hoarefroſt, ſnow shal fal vpon them.
\v 17 At the time, when they shal be diſſipated they shal perish: and after they waxe hote they shal be diſſolued out of their place.
\v 18 The pathes of their ſteppes are intangled: they shal walke in vayne, and shal perish.
\v 19 Coſider ye the pathes \f b \fr 6.19 \ft Sandie wayes without water.\f* of Thema, the wayes of Saba, & expect a litle while.
\v 20 They are confounded, becauſe I haue hoped: they are come alſo euen vnto me, and are couered with shame.
\v 21 Now you are come: and euen now ſeing my plague you are afrayd.
\v 22 haue I ſayd: Bring ye to me, and of your ſubſtance geue to me?
\v 23 Or deliuer me from the hand of the enemie, and out of the hand of the ſtrong deliuer me?
\v 24 \f c \fr 6.24 \ft Iobs aduerſaries preſumed to teach him, but becauſe they erred in theit applying of true aſſertions in his caſe, which himſelf vnderſtood and not they, he acconted not their diſcourſes for good doctrin.\f* Teach ye me, and I wil hold my peace: and if I perhaps haue beene ignorant in anie thing, inſtruct ye me.
\v 25 Why haue you detracted from the wordes of truth, whereas there is none of you that can controwle me?
\v 26 To rebuke only you frame ſpeaches, and you vtter wordes in the wind
\v 27 You rush in vpon a pupil, and you endeuoure to ouerthrow your frend.
\v 28 Notwithſtanding accomplish that which you haue begune: geue eare, and ſee whether I lie.
\v 29 Anſwer I beſech you without contention: and ſpeaking that which is iuſt, iudge ye.
\v 30 And you shal not finde iniquitie in my tongue, neither shal folie ſound in my iawes.
\c 7
\cd Iob explicateth diuers calamities of mans life, and namely of his owne. 6. Suppoſing it not likelie that he shal returne to former proſperous ſtate, 15. deſireth to dye.
\p
\v 1 THE life of man vpon earth is a \f a \fr 7.1 \ft A ſouldiar muſt be alwayes readie to indure trauel, to be promptly obedient, content to be beaten by his ſuperior without al reſiſtance, vpon paine of his life, he is alwayes ſubiect to cares, and to danger of death, and therfore muſt euer be readie to dye.\f* warfare, & his daies, as the daies of an hyred man.
\v 2 As a ſeruant deſireth the shadow, & as the hired man taryeth for the end of his worke:
\v 3 So I alſo haue had vayne monethes, and haue numbred to my ſelf laborious nightes.
\v 4 If I ſleepe, I shal ſay: When shal I ariſe? and agayne I shal expect the euening, and shal be replenished with ſorowes euen vntil darkenens.
\v 5 My flesh is clothed with rotteneſſe and filth of duſt, my skinne is withered, & drawen together.
\v 6 My daies haue paſſed more ſwiftly, then the webbe is cut of the weauer, and are conſumed without anie hope.
\v 7 Remember that my life is a winde, and myne eie shal not returne to ſee good thinges.
\v 8 Neither shal the ſight of man behold me: thine eies vpon me, and I shal not ſtand.
\v 9 As a clowde is conſumed, and paſſeth away: ſo he that shal deſcend to \f 1 \fr 7.9 \ft \+it Seol\+it*.\f* hel shal not aſcend.
\v 10 Neither shal he returne anie more into his houſe, neither shal his place know him anie more.
\v 11 Wherfore I alſo wil not ſpare my mouth, I wil ſpeake in the tribulation of my ſpirit: I wil talke with the bitterneſſe of my ſoule.
\v 12 Why, am I a ſea, or a whale, that thou haſt compaſſed me with a priſon.
\v 13 If I ſay: My litle bed shal confort me, and I shal be releeued ſpeaking with myſelf in my couch:
\v 14 Thou wilt terrefie me by dreames, and by viſions shake me with horrour.
\v 15 For the which thing my ſoule hath choſen hanging, and my bones death.
\v 16 I haue deſpayred. I shal now liue no longer: \f b \fr 7.16 \ft Iuſt Iob ſuppoſing he was at the point of death, prayed God to ſpare or \+it ceaſe to\+it* punish him more, and to accept of that affliction which he had already ſuffered. So the Church in behalfe of ſoules departed in ſtate of grace prayeth God to ſpare and ceaſe from further punishing them, and to geue them eternal reſt.\f* Spare me, for my daies are nothing.
\v 17 What is man that thou magnifieſt him? or why ſetteſt thou thy hart toward him?
\v 18 Thou doeſt viſite him early, and ſodenly thou proueſt him:
\v 19 How long doeſt thou not ſpare me, nor ſuffer me that I ſwallow my ſpitle?
\v 20 I haue ſinned, what shal I doe to thee ô keeper of men? why haſt thou ſette me contrarie to thee, and I am become burdenous to my ſelf?
\v 21 Why doeſt thou not take away my ſinne, and why doeſt thou not take away mine iniquitie? Behold now I shal ſleepe in the duſt: and if thou ſeeke me in the morning, I shal not be.
\c 8
\cd Baldad chargeth Iob to haue ſpoken, vniuſtly exhorting him to turne to God, and ſo he shal proſper better then heretofore. 13. shewing that hypocrites shal not proſper, 20. inferreth (falſly) that God afflicteth not the ſincere, nor helpeth the malignant.
\p
\v 1 BVT Baldad the Suhite anſwering, ſayd: \f a \fr 8.1 \ft The ſecond conflict. This ſecond diſputer charged Iob to be obſtinate who in dede was conſtant in a true ſetled iudgement.\f*
\v 2 How long wilt thou ſpeake ſuch thinges, and shal the ſpirit of the word of thy mouth be multiplied?
\v 3 Why doth God ſupplant iugement? or doth the Omnipotent ſubuert that which is iuſt?
\v 4 Although thy children haue ſinned to him, and he hath leaft them in the hand of their iniquitie:
\v 5 Yet if thou wilt ariſe early to God, and wilt beſech the Omnipotent:
\v 6 If thou wilt walke cleane & vpright, he wil forthwith awake vnto thee, and wil make the habitation of thy iuſtice peaceable:
\v 7 In ſo much, that if thy former thinges haue bene litle, thy later thinges may be multipled excedingly.
\v 8 For aske the old generation, and ſearch diligently the memorie of the fathers.
\v 9 (For we are but as yeſterday, and are ignorant \f b \fr 8.9 \ft Euen thus heretikes imagine Catholikes to the ignorant, and therfore fil their mouthes and bookes with thinges that are not denied, and yet inferre much falshood ſophiſtically applying one truth againſt an other being them ſelues ignorant how to reconcile difficulties.\f* that our daies vpon the earth are as a shadow.)
\v 10 And they shal teach thee: they shal ſpeake to thee; and from their hart shal vtter wordes.
\v 11 Can the rush be greene without moyſture? or a ſeggie place grow without water?
\v 12 When it is yet in his flowre, and is not plucked with the hand, it witereth before al hearbes:
\v 13 Euen ſo the waies of al, that forget God, and the hope of the hypocrite shal perish:
\v 14 His folie shal not pleaſe him, and his confidence as the ſpiders webbe.
\v 15 He shal leane vpon his houſe, and it shal not ſtand: he shal ſtay it vp, and it shal not riſe:
\v 16 He ſemeth moyſtened before the ſunne come, and in his riſing his bloſſome shal goe forth.
\v 17 Vpon a heape of rockes his rootes shal be thicke, and among ſtones he shal abide.
\v 18 If he ſwallow him vp out of his place, he wil denie him, & wil ſay: I know thee not.
\v 19 For this is the ioy of of his way, that others may ſpring againe of the earth.
\v 20 God wil not reiect the ſimple, nor reach his hand to the malignant.
\v 21 Vntil thy mouth be filled with laughter, and thy lippes with iubilation.
\v 22 They that hate thee, shal be clothed with confuſion: and the tabernacle of the impious shal not ſtand.
\c 9
\cd Iob approueth that no man auouching his owne iuſtice before God is iuſtified. 22. Teacheth that affliction of the innocent ſtandeth wel with Gods iuſtice, wiſdom, and powre.
\p
\v 1 AND Iob anſwering, ſaid:
\v 2 \f a \fr 9.2 \ft Iob here granteth that which was truly ſaid by his aduerſarie, & ſheweth how he did wrong aplie true doctrin againſt him, and ſo ſtil defendeth his owne innocencie, and iuſt quarel\f* In deede I know it is ſo, & that man can not be iuſtified compared with God
\v 3 If he wil contend with him, he can not anſwer him one for a thouſand.
\v 4 He is wiſe of hart, and ſtrong of force: who hath reſiſted him, & hath had peace?
\v 5 He that tranſported mountaynes, and they whom he ſubuerted in his furie, knew not.
\v 6 He that remoueth the earth out of her place, and the pillers therof are shaken.
\v 7 He that commandeth the ſunne, & it riſeth not: and shutteth vp the ſtarres as it were vnder a ſeale:
\v 8 He that alone ſpreadeth the heauens, and goeth vpon the waues of the ſea.
\v 9 He that maketh Arcturus, and Orion, and Hyades, and the inner partes of the ſouth.
\v 10 He that doth great thinges, and incomprehenſible, and meruelous, of the which there is no number.
\v 11 If he come to me, I shal not ſee him: if he depart, I shal not vnderſtand.
\v 12 If ſodenly he aske, who shal anſwer him? or who can ſay: Why doeſt thou ſo?
\v 13 God whoſe wrath no man can reſiſt, and vnder whom they ſtoope that \f b \fr 9.13 \ft Angeles moue the ſpheres of heauen.\f* carie the world
\v 14 How great am I then, that I may anſwer him, and ſpeake in my wordes with him?
\v 15 Who although I haue anie iuſt thing, wil not anſwer, \f c \fr 9.15 \ft Euen ſo S. Paul, though he was not guiltie in conſcience of anie crime, yet he would not therin iuſtifie himſelf. \+it 1. Cor. 4\+it*.\f* but wil beſech my iudge
\v 16 And when he shal heare me invocating, I doe not beleue that he hath heard my voice.
\v 17 For in a hurlewinde shal he breake me, and shal multiplie my woundes yea without cauſe.
\v 18 He graunteth not my ſpirit to reſt, and he filleth me with bitterneſſe.
\v 19 If ſtrength be demaunded, he is moſt ſtrong: if equitie of iudgement, not man dare geue teſtimonie for me.
\v 20 If I wil iuſtifie my ſelf, mine owne mouth shal condemne me, if I wil shew my ſelf innocent, he shal proue me wicked.
\v 21 Although I shal be ſimple, the ſelf ſame shal my ſoul be ignorant of, and I shal be wearie of my life.
\v 22 \f d \fr 9.22 \ft This is an aſſured true aſſertion, that God afflicteth both good & euil in this life.\f* One thing there is that I haue ſpoken, both the innocent and the impious he conſumeth
\v 23 If he ſcourge, let him kil at once, and not laugh at the payns of innocentes.
\v 24 The earth is geuen into the handes of the impious, he couereth the face of the iudges therof: and if it be not he, who is it then?
\v 25 My dayes haue bene ſwifter then a poſtè: they haue fled and haue not ſene good.
\v 26 They haue paſſed by as shippes carying fruites, as an eagle flying to meate,
\v 27 When I shal ſay: I wil not ſpeake ſo, I change my face, and am tormented with ſorow.
\v 28 I \f e \fr 9.28 \ft Feare on mans part, and hope in God, do wel conſiſt together. So both preſumption and deſperation are auoided.\f* feared al my workes, knowing that thou didſt not ſpare the offender.
\v 29 But if ſo alſo I am impious, why haue I laboured in vayne?
\v 30 If I be waſhed as it were with ſnow waters, and my handes shal. shine as moſt cleane:
\v 31 Yet shal thou dippe me in filth, and my garmentes ſhal abhorre me.
\v 32 For neither I wil anſwer a man that is like my ſelf: nor that may be heard with me equally in iudgement.
\v 33 There is none that may be able to reproue both, and to put his hand betwen both.
\v 34 Let him take his rod from me, and let not his dread terrifie me.
\v 35 I wil ſpeake, and wil not feare him: for I can not anſwer fearing.
\c 10
\cd Iob ſcarſe able to ſpeake yet sheweth that there is no iniuſtice nor ignorance in God, neither is his ſinne the cauſe of ſo great afflictions. 9. Acknowledgeth Gods loue and benefites towards himſelf. 15. and dreadeth his ſtrict iudgement.
\p
\v 1 MY ſoule is wearie of my life, I wil let my ſpeach paſſe agaynſt my ſelf. I wil ſpeake in the bitternes of my ſoule.
\v 2 I wil ſay to God: Condemne me not: tel me why thou iudgeſt me ſo.
\v 3 Doth it ſeeme good to thee, if thou \f a \fr 10.3 \ft Holie Iob knowing it to be vnpoſſible that God calumpiateth anie man, inquireth what is the cauſe why his goodnes afflicteth the iuſt?\f* calumniate me, and oppreſſe me the worke of thy handes, and helpe the counſel of the impious?
\v 4 Haſt thou eies of flesh: or as a man ſeeth, shalt thou alſo ſee?
\v 5 Are thy daies as the daies of man, and are thy yeares as the times of men:
\v 6 That thou ſekeſt my iniquitie, and ſearcheſt my ſinne?
\v 7 And thou mayſt knowe that I haue done no impious thing, whereas there is no man that can deliuer out of thy hand.
\v 8 \f b \fr 10.8 \ft In that I am thy creature thou maiſt iuſtly deſtroy me: but in deede becauſe I am thy creature thou wilt ſpare me through thy mercy, geuing me thy grace which if I vſe wel thou wilt alſo geue me eternal reſt.\f* Thy handes haue made me, and framed me wholly round about, and doſt thou ſo ſodenlie caſt me downe headlong?
\v 9 Remember I beſech thee that as clay thou madeſt me, and into duſt thou wilt bring me agayne.
\v 10 Haſt thou not as milke milked me, and curded me as cheeſe?
\v 11 With skinne and flesh thou haſt clothed me: wirh bones & ſinowes thou haſt compacted me.
\v 12 Life and mercie thou haſt geuen to me, and thy viſitation hath kept my ſpirit.
\v 13 Although thou conceale theſe thinges in thy hart, yet I know that thou remembreſt al thinges.
\v 14 If I haue ſinned and thou haſt ſpared me for an houre: why doeſt thou not ſuffer me to be cleane from mine iniquttie?
\v 15 And if I shal be impious, woe is to me: and if iuſt, I shal not lift vp my head, filled with affliction and miſerie.
\v 16 And for pride as a lioneſſe thou wilt take me, and returning thou doeſt meruelouſly torment me.
\v 17 Thou reneweſt thy witneſſes agaynſt me, and multiplieſt thy wrath toward me, and paynes doe warre vpon me.
\v 18 Why didſt thou bring me forth out of the matrice? Who would God, I had beene conſumed that eye might not ſee me.
\v 19 I had beene as if I were not, caried from the wombe to the graue.
\v 20 Shal not the fewnes of my daies be ended ſhortly? ſuffer me \f c \fr 10.20 \ft Repentance is alwayes neceſſarie, but moſt eſpecially at the houre of death.\f* therfore, that I may a litle lament my ſorow:
\v 21 Before I goe, and returne not, vnto the darke land, that is couered with the miſt of death:
\v 22 A land of miſerie and darkeneſſe, where is the shadow of death, and no order, but euerlaſting horrour inhabiteth.
\c 11
\cd Sophar imputeth Iobs diſcourſe, about the cauſe of his ſo great afflictions, to inſolencie of mind, and loquaſitie of tongue, perſwading him to acknowlege greuous ſinnes, that ſo he may haue the reward of a iuſt man. \f - \ft The third conflict.\f*
\p
\v 1 BVt ſophar the Naamathite anſwering, ſaid:
\v 2 Why, ſhal he that ſpeaketh manie thinges, not heare alſo? or \f a \fr 11.2 \ft Sophar might haue applied the vice of much ſpeaking to himſelf, and his felowes, alleaging manie thinges, which proued not their opinion, wheras Iobs allegations proued directly that which he affirmed.\f* shal a man ful of wordes be iuſtified?
\v 3 To thee onlie shal men hold their peace? and when thou haſt mocked others, shalt thou be confuted of none?
\v 4 For thou haſt ſayd: My word is pure, and I am cleane in thy ſight.
\v 5 And I would wish that God would ſpeake with thee, and would open his lippes to thee,
\v 6 That he might shew thee the ſecretes of wiſdom, and that his law is manifold, and thou mighteſt vnderſtand that thou art exacted much leſſer thinges of him, \f b \fr 11.6 \ft Iobs owne conſcience affirmed the contrarie. \+it c.6.v.3\+it*.\f* then thy iniquitie deſerueth.
\v 7 Peraduenture thou wilt comprehend the ſteppes of God, and wil find out the Omnipotent perfectly?
\v 8 He is higher then heauen, and what wilt thou doe: deeper then hel, and how wilt thou know?
\v 9 The meaſure of him is longer then the earth, and broder then the ſea.
\v 10 If he shal ouerthrow al things, or shal ſtrayten them into one, who shal ſay againſt him?
\v 11 For he knoweth the vanitie of men, & ſeing iniquity doth he not cõſider?
\v 12 A vaine man is extolled into pride, and thinketh himſelf borne free as a wilde aſſes colt.
\v 13 But thou haſt confirmed thy hart, & haſt ſpred thy handes to him.
\v 14 If thou shalt take away from thee the iniquitie \f c \fr 11.14 \ft He could not iuſtly confeſſe iniquitie which he had not committed,\f* that is in thy hand, and iniuſtice remaine not in thy tabernacle:
\v 15 Then mayſt thou lift vp thy face wthout ſpotte, and thou shalt, be ſtable, and shalt not feare.
\v 16 Thou shalt alſo forget miſerie, and shalt remember it as waters that are paſſed.
\v 17 And the brightnes as it were of noone daies, shal ariſe to thee at euening: and when thou shalt think thyſelf conſumed, thou shalt riſe as the day ſtarre.
\v 18 And thou shalt haue confidence, hope being ſet before thee, and buried thou shalt ſleepe ſecure.
\v 19 Thou shalt reſt, and there shal be none to terrifie thee: and verie manie shal beſech thy face.
\v 20 But the eies of the impious shal decay, and eſcape shal faile them, and their hope the abomination of the ſoule.
\c 12
\cd Iob sheweth the knowlege, which his freinds much boaſt of, to be the common knowne doctrin of Gods ſeruantes. He more truly, and more profoundly diſcourſeth of Gods powre and wiſdome, ſtil defending his owne innocencie in reſpect of great ſinnes.
\p
\v 1 BVt Iob anſwering, ſayd:
\v 2 Are you then men alone, & shal wiſedome die with you?
\v 3 I alſo haue a hart euen as you, neither am I inferiour to you: for who is ignorãt of theſe thinges, which you know?
\v 4 He that is mocked of his frend as I, shal inuocate God & he wil heare him: for the \f a \fr 12.4 \ft God ſuffereth his ſimple true meaning ſeruantes to be ſcorned for the time, but afterward the wicked shal be forced to confeſſe, that thoſe whom they derided are worthily in honour before God. \+it Sap. 5.v.3\+it*.\f* ſimplicitie of the iuſt man is ſcorned.
\v 5 The lampe contemned in the cogitations of the riche, is prepared to the time appointed.
\v 6 The tabernacles of robbers abound, & they prouoke God bouldly, wheras he hath geuen al thinges into their handes.
\v 7 For aske the beaſtes, and they shal teach thee: and the foules of the ayre, and they shal tel thee.
\v 8 Speake to the earth, and it shal anſwer thee, and the fishes of the ſea shal tel.
\v 9 Who is ignorant that the hand of our Lord hath made al theſe things?
\v 10 In whoſe hand is the ſoule of euerie liuing thing, and the ſpirit of al the flesh of man.
\v 11 Doth not the eare diſcerne wordes, and the iawes of him that eateth, the taſt?
\v 12 In the ancientes is wiſedom, and in long time prudence.
\v 13 With him is wiſedom and ſtrength, he hath counſel and vnderſtanding.
\v 14 If he shal deſtroy, there is no man that can build: if he shut vp a man, there is none that can open.
\v 15 If he hold in the waters, al things shal be dried: and if he ſend them forth, they shal ouerthrow the earth.
\v 16 With him is ſtrength and wiſedom: he knoweth both thc deceiuer, & him that is deceiued.
\v 17 He bringeth \f b \fr 12.17 \ft Craftie plotters of deuiſes at laſt by Gods iuſt punishement commit ſo great abſurdites, that the meaneſt may ſee their follie.\f* counſelers to a folish end, and iudges to aſtonishment.
\v 18 He looſeth the belt of kings, and girdeth their reines with a corde.
\v 19 He leadeth away Prieſtes without glorie, and ſupplanteth the great men.
\v 20 Changing the lippe of the true, and taking away the doctrine of the ancientes.
\v 21 He powreth out contempt vpon princes, releeuing them that had bene oppreſſed.
\v 22 Who reueleth profound things out of darkeneſſe, and bringeth forth the shadow of death into light.
\v 23 Who multiplieth nations, and deſtroyeth them, and reſtoreth the deſtroyed whole agayne.
\v 24 Who changeth the hart of the princes of the people of the earth, and deceiueth them, that they may goe in vayne where is no paſſage.
\v 25 They shal grope as in the darke, and not in the light, and he shal make them goe amis as druncken men.
\c 13
\cd Of their owne wordes Iob confuteth his aduerſaries, that they haue ſpoken that which they know not. 13. defendeth his owne innocencie. 22. deſiring of God, if he be afflicted for ſecrete ſinnes, that he may know them.
\p
\v 1 BEHOLD mine eie hath ſeene al theſe thinges, and mine eare hath heard, & I haue vnderſtood euerie thing.
\v 2 According to your knowledge I alſo do know: neither am I inferiour to you.
\v 3 But yet I wil ſpeake to the Omnipotent, and I couet to diſpute with God.
\v 4 Firſt ſhewing you to be forgers of lying, and mainteyners of peruerſe opinions.
\v 5 And would God ye would hold your peace, that you might be thought to be wiſe men.
\v 6 Heare ye therfore my correptions, and attend the iudgement of my lippes.
\v 7 Hath God neede of your lye, that for him you ſpeake guiles.
\v 8 Doe you take his perſon, and doe you endeuour \f a \fr 13.8 \ft Iobs ſtate of ſinne or innocencie was beſt knowen to God, next to his owne conſcience, not at al to his aduerſaries, that preſumed to iudge therof.\f* to iudge for God?
\v 9 Or shal it pleaſe him from whom nothing can be concealed, or shal he be deceiued as a man, with your fraudulent dealings?
\v 10 He shal reproue you, becauſe in ſecrete you take his perſon.
\v 11 Forthwith as he shal moue himſelf, he shal truble you: and his terrour ſhal come violently vpon you.
\v 12 Your memorie shal be compared to ashes, and your neckes shal be brought into clay.
\v 13 Hold your peace a litle while, that I may ſpeake, what ſoeuer my minde shal prompt me.
\v 14 \f b \fr 13.14 \ft It ſemed to his aduerſaries that of deſperation he would tear his flesh, and ſo kil himſelf, and to be ſo nere death as if one held his ſoule in his hand readie to bet it fal from him.\f* Why doe I teare my flesh with my teeth, & carie my ſoule in my handes?
\v 15 Although he shal kil me, I wil truſt in him: but yet I wil reproue my waies in his ſight.
\v 16 And he shal be my ſauiour: for no hypocrite shal come in his ſight.
\v 17 Heare ye my word, and receiue the obſcure ſayings with your eares.
\v 18 If I shal be iudged, \f c \fr 13.18 \ft He was in extreme anguish, but ſtil truſted in God.\f* I know that I shal be found iuſt.
\v 19 What is he that wil be iudged with me? let him come: why am I conſumed holding my peace?
\v 20 Two things only do not to me, and then shal I not be hid from thy face:
\v 21 Make thy hand far from me, and let not thy feare terrifie me.
\v 22 Cal me, and I wil anſwer thee: or els I wil ſpeake, and doe thou anſwer me.
\v 23 How great iniquites and ſinnes I haue, my wicked deedes and my offences \f d \fr 13.23 \ft He denieth that he iſ guiltie in conſcience but deſireth to know of God, if he haue anie hid ſinnes which himſelf knoweth not.\f* shewe thou me.
\v 24 Why hideſt thou thy face, and thinkeſt me thine enemy?
\v 25 Againſt the leafe, that is violently taken with the wind, thou sheweſt thy might, and perſecuteſt drie ſtuble.
\v 26 For thou writeſt bitternes againſt me, and wilt conſume me with the ſinnes of my youth.
\v 27 Thou haſt put my feete in the ſtockes, and haſt obſerued al my pathes, and haſt conſidered the ſteppes of my feete.
\v 28 Who as rottenes am to be conſumed, and as a garment, that is eaten of the moth.
\c 14
\cd Againe Iob deſcribeth the miſeries of mans life. 3. Neuertheles Gods great prouidence towards him. 7. profeſſeth his beleefe of the Reſurrection.
\p
\v 1 MAN borne of \f a \fr 14.1 \ft Al children taking their ſubſtance from the mother, and hauing only a temporal life, continually tending to death, can not but be ſubiect to manie miſeries.: For time it ſelfe though it may ſeme long, is a limited thing, and is very ſhorte, yea hath no proportion, in reſpect of eternitie, which is infinite yet God hath care to bring this weake creature to life eternal.\f* woman, liuing a short time, is replenished with many mſeiries.
\v 2 Who as a flowre cometh forth and is deſtroyed, & fleeth as a shadow, & neuer abideth in the ſame ſtate.
\v 3 And doeſt thou counte it a worthy thing to open thine eies vpon ſuch an one, and to bring him with thee into iudgement?
\v 4 Who can make cleane him that is conceiued of vncleane ſeede? is it not thou which onlie art?
\v 5 The daies of man are short, & the number of his monethes is with thee, thou haſt appointed his limittes which can not be paſſed.
\v 6 Depart a litle from him, that he may reſt, vntil his day wished for, come, euen as the hyred man.
\v 7 A tree hath hope: if it be cut, it waxeth greene againe, and the boughes thereof ſpring.
\v 8 If his roote be old in the earth, and the truncke therof be dead in the duſt.
\v 9 At the ſent of water it shal ſpring, and bring forth leaues, as when it was firſt planted.
\v 10 But when man shal be dead, and naked and conſumed, where is he I pray?
\v 11 As if the waters should deparr out of the ſea, and a riuer made emptie should be dried vp.
\v 12 So man when he is a ſleepe shal not riſe agayne, til heauen perish, he shal not awake, nor riſe vp out of his ſleepe.
\v 13 Who wil grant me this, that in hel thou protect me, and hide me, til thy furie paſſe, and appoynt me a time, wherin thou wilt remember me?
\v 14 Shal man that is dead, thinkeſt thou, liue agayne? al the daies, in which I am now in warfare, I expect vntil my change do come.
\v 15 Thou shalt cal me, and I shal anſwer thee: to the worke of thy handes thou shalt reach thy right hand.
\v 16 Thou in dede haſt numbred my ſteppes: but thou wilt ſpare my ſinnes.
\v 17 Thou haſt ſealed my offences as it were in a bag, but haſt cured mine iniquitie.
\v 18 A mountaine falling ſlideth downe, and a rock is remoued out of his place.
\v 19 Waters make ſtones holow, and with inundation the earth by litle and litle is conſumed: and men therfore thou shalt deſtroy in like maner.
\v 20 Thou haſt ſtrengthened him a litle that he might paſſe away foreuer: thou shalt chãge his face, and shalt ſend him forth.
\v 21 Whether his children shal be noble, or vnnoble, he \f b \fr 14.21 \ft The parentes after death are not afflicted with the ſtate of their children, as they be in this life.\f* shal not vnderſtand.
\v 22 But yet his flesh whiles he shal liue shal haue ſorow, & his ſoule shal mourne vpon himſelf.
\c 15
\cd Eliphaz againe chargeth Iob to haue ſpoken preſumptuouſly & blaſphemouſly. 14. auoucheth that no man is innocent nor iuſt. 20. deſcribing the malediction of impious and hypochrites.
\p
\v 1 BVT \f 1 \fr 15.1 \ft The fourt conflict,\f* Eliphaz the Themanite anſwering, ſayd:
\v 2 Wil a wiſe man anſwer as it were ſpeaking into the wind, and fil his ſtomacke with burning?
\v 3 Thou \f a \fr 15.3 \ft Eliphaz not able to anſwer Iobs ſolide reaſons, raileth againſt him, as if he were iniurious to God, or taught others to caſt away feare,\f* reproueſt him in wordes, that is not equal to thee, and ſpeakeſt that which is not expedient for thee.
\v 4 As much as is in thee, thou haſt euacuated feare, and haſt taken away prayers before God.
\v 5 For thine iniquitie hath taught thy mouth, and thou doeſt imitate the tongue of blaſphemers.
\v 6 Thine owne mouth shal condemne thee, and not I: and thy lippes shal anſwer thee.
\v 7 Waſt thou the firſt man borne, and formed before the litle hilles?
\v 8 Haſt thou heard Gods counſel, and shal his wiſedome be inferiour to thee?
\v 9 What doeſt thou know, that we are ignorant of? what doeſt thou vnderſtand that we know not?
\v 10 There are both oldmen and ancientes among vs, much elder then thy fathers.
\v 11 Is it a great matter that God should comforth thee: but thy naughtie wordes hinder it.
\v 12 Why doth thy hart eleuate thee, and as thinking great thinges, haſt thou eſtonied eies.
\v 13 Why doeth thy ſpirit ſwel agaynſt God, to vtter ſuch wordes out of thy mouth.
\v 14 What is man, that he should be \f b \fr 15.14 \ft It is a very rare priuilege to be without ſpot.\f* without ſpot, and that the borne of a woman should appeare \f c \fr 15.14 \ft But ſpot of venial ſinnes may be in a iuſt man.\f* iuſt?
\v 15 Behold among his ſainctes none is immutable, and the heauens are not cleane in his ſight.
\v 16 How much more is man abominable, and vnprofitable, who drinketh iniquitie as it were water?
\v 17 I wil shew thee, heare me: that which I haue ſeene I wil tel thee.
\v 18 Wiſemen confeſſe and hide not their fathers.
\v 19 To whom onlie the earth was geuen, and ſtranger hath not paſſed by them.
\v 20 The impious is proud al his daies, and \f d \fr 15.20 \ft Al theſe miſeries are incident to the wicked, but are falſly applied to holie Iob, who in dede was iuſt.\f* the number of the yeares of his tyrannie is vncertaine.
\v 21 The ſound of terrour is alwaies in his eares: and when there is peace, he alwaies ſuſpecteth treaſon.
\v 22 He beleueth not that he may returne from darkeneſſe to light, looking round about for the ſword on euerie ſide.
\v 23 When he shal moue himſelf to ſeeke bread, he knoweth that the day of darkeneſſe is prepared in his hand.
\v 24 Tribulation shal terrifie him, and diſtreſſe shal compaſſe him, as a king that is prepared to battel.
\v 25 For he hath ſtretched his hand againſt God, and is ſtrengthened againſt the Omnipotent.
\v 26 He hath runne againſt him with necke ſet vp right, and is armed with a fatte necke.
\v 27 Fatneſſe hath couered his face, and from his ſides there hangeth tallow.
\v 28 He hath dwelt in deſolate cities, and in deſert houſes, that are brought into hillockes.
\v 29 He shal not be enriched, neither shal his ſubſtance continew, neither shal he put his roote in the earth.
\v 30 He shal not depart out of darkenes, the flame shal drie his boughes, and he shal be taken away wirh the ſpirit of his owne mouth.
\v 31 He shal not beleeue vainely deceiued with errour, that he may be redemed with anie price.
\v 32 Before his daies be accomplished, he shal perish: and his handes shal wither.
\v 33 His cluſter in the firſt flower shal be hurt as a vine, and as the oliue tree caſting his flower.
\v 34 For the congregation of the hypocrite is barren, and fire shal deuoure their tabernacles, which gladly take giftes.
\v 35 He conceiued ſorow, and hath brought forth iniquitie, and his wombe preprareth guiles.
\c 16
\cd Iob moued by his importune freindes, 4. expoſtulateth their ſeueritie, 12. further deſcribeth his afflictions, and appealeth to Gods iudgement, that he ſuffereth more then his ſinnes deſerue.
\p
\v 1 BVT Iob anſwering ſayd:
\v 2 I haue heard often times ſuch things, \f a \fr 16.2 \ft True and freindlie comforters ought to heare the afflicted with patience, and not vnmercifully charge him with crimes which they neither know, nor his conſcience is guiltie of.\f* heauie conforters you are al.
\v 3 Shal wordes ful of winde haue an end? or is anie thing trubleſome to thee, if thou ſpeake?
\v 4 I alſo could ſpeake thinges like to you: and would God your ſoule were for my ſoule.
\v 5 I alſo would confort you with wordes, and would wag my head vpon you.
\v 6 I would ſtrengthen you with my mouth, and would moue my lippes, as ſparing you.
\v 7 But what shal I doe? If I ſpeake, my paine wil not reſt: and if I hold my peace, it wil not depart from me.
\v 8 But now my ſorow hath oppreſſed me, and al my limmes are brought to nothing.
\v 9 My wrinkles giue teſtimonie againſt me, and \f b \fr 16.9 \ft A great affliction, when one ful of paine and diſtres is alſo forced to defend his owne innocencie againſt calumniators.\f* a falſe ſpeaker is rayſed vp againſt my face contradicting me.
\v 10 He hath gathered his furie vpon me, and threatening me hath gnashed againſt me with his teeth, mine enemy hath beheld me with terrible eies.
\v 11 They haue opened their mouthes vpon me, and exprobating haue ſtrooken my cheke, they are filled with my paines.
\v 12 God hath shut me vp with the wicked man, and hath deliuered me to the hands of the impious.
\v 13 I ſometime that welthie one ſodenly am broken: he hath held my necke, broken me, and ſet me to himſelf as it were a marke.
\v 14 He hath compaſſed me with his ſpeares, he hath wounded my loynes, he hath not ſpared, and hath powred out on the earth my bowels.
\v 15 He hath cut me with wound vpon wound, he hath come violently vpon me as it were a giant.
\v 16 I haue ſowed ſackcloth vpon my skinne, and haue couered my flesh with ashes.
\v 17 My face is ſwollen with weeping, and my eyeliddes are dimme.
\v 18 Theſe thinges haue I ſuffred \f c \fr 16.18 \ft As the aduerſaries ſtil obiect great iniquitie to him ſo he yeldeth them the ſame true anſwer.\f* without the iniquitie of my hand, wheras I had cleane prayers to God.
\v 19 Earth couer not my bloud, neither let my crie find place in thee to be hid.
\v 20 For behold my witneſſe is in heauen, and he that knoweth my conſcience on high.
\v 21 My freindes ful of wordes: mine eie diſtilleth vnto God.
\v 22 And would God a man might ſo be iudged with God, as the ſonne of man is iudged with his companion.
\v 23 For behold the short yeares paſſe away, and I walke the path, by the which I shal not returne.
\c 17
\cd For the greuouſnes of his paine Iob expecteth ſpeedie death, 4. chargeth his freindes with folie for holding only remuneration in this life. 6. himſelf hopeth happie reſt in the other world.
\p
\v 1 MY ſpirit shal be extenuated, my daies shal be shortened, and the graue only remaineth for me.
\v 2 I haue \f a \fr 17.2 \ft Not hauing committed ſuch ſinnes as he was charged withal, in this duble bitternes of corporal paine and calumniation, his eye was ſtil vpon God, expecting to be deliuered.\f* not ſinned, and mine eie abideth in bitterneſſe.
\v 3 Deliuer me, and ſet me beſide thee, and let anie mans hand fight againſt me.
\v 4 Thou haſt made their hart far from diſcipline, and therfore they shal not be exalted.
\v 5 He promiſeth a praye to his felowes, and the eies of his children shal faile.
\v 6 He hath ſet me as it were for a prouerbe of the comon people, and I am an example before them.
\v 7 Mine eie is dimne for indignation, and my members are brought as it were to nothing.
\v 8 The iuſt shal be aſtonied vpon this, and the innocent shal be raiſed vp againſt the hypocrite.
\v 9 And the iuſt shal hold his way, and with cleane handes shal adde ſtrength.
\v 10 Be al you therfore conuerted, and come, and I shal not finde among you anie wiſeman.
\v 11 My daies haue paſſed, my cogitations are diſſipated, tormenting my hart:
\v 12 Night they haue turned into day, and againe after darkeneſſe I hope for light.
\v 13 If I shal expect, \f 1 \fr 17.13 \ft \+it Seol Ἀδὴς Infernus.\+it*\f* \f b \fr 17.13 \ft This text sheweth euidently that there was a place of reſt called hel.\f* hel is my houſe, and in darkeneſſe I haue made my bed.
\v 14 I haue ſayd to rottenes: Thou art my father, my mother, and my ſiſter, to wormes.
\v 15 Where is now then my expectation, and my patience who conſidereth.
\v 16 Al my thinges shal deſcend into moſt deepe hel: there at the leaſt, shal I haue reſt thinkeſt thou?
\c 18
\cd Baldad ſetteth vpon Iob againe, chargeing him with preſent impatience, and former impietie, 6. and that therfore he ſuffereth worthie punishment. \f - \ft The fifth conflict.\f*
\p
\v 1 BVT Baldad the Suhite anſwering, ſayd:
\v 2 How long wil \f a \fr 18.2 \ft Baldad perceiuing Iob to ſpeake confidently as the common doctrin of manie oppoſeth him ſelf neuertheles againſt him and al that thincke as he doth, and ſo ſpeaketh as to manie in the plural number, \+it vvil ye ſpeake\+it* and of himſelfe & his felowes: \+it are we reputed\+it*.&c. So holie Iob a figure of the Church defended the common cauſe, his aduerſaries a figure of heretikes ſpeaking ſome truthes mixed falſe thinges therwith \+it s. Greg. li. 14. c. 1\+it*.\f* ye ſpeake vaunting wordes? vnderſtand ye firſt, and ſo let vs ſpeake.
\v 3 Why are we reputed as beaſtes, and accounted vile before you?
\v 4 Which deſtroyeſt thy ſoule in thy furie, shal the earth be forſaken for thee, and shal rockes be tranſported out of their place?
\v 5 Shal not the light of the impious be extinguished, and the flame of his fire not shine?
\v 6 The light shal be darke in his tabernacle, and the candel that is ouer him, shal be extinguished.
\v 7 The ſteppes of his power shal be ſtraytened, and his owne counſel shal caſt him downe headlong.
\v 8 For he hath thruſt his feete into a nette, and walketh in the mashes therof.
\v 9 The ſole of his foote shal be held in a ſnare, and thirſt shal burne againſt him.
\v 10 His ginne is hid in the earth, and his ſpring vpon the path.
\v 11 Feares shal terrifie him on euerie ſide, and his feete shal entangle him.
\v 12 Let his ſtrength be extenuated with famine, and let hunger inuade his ribbes.
\v 13 Let it deuoure the beautie of his skinne, let death the firſtborne conſume his armes.
\v 14 Let his confidence be plucked away out of his tabernacle, and let deſtruction as a king tread vpon him.
\v 15 Let the companious of him, that is not, dwel in his tabernacle, let brinſton be ſprinkled in his tent.
\v 16 Let his rootes be dried downward, and his harueſt deſtroyed vpward.
\v 17 Let the memorie of him perish from the earth, and let not his name be renowmed in the ſtreates.
\v 18 He shal expel him out of light into darkeneſſe, and shal tranſport him out of the world.
\v 19 His ſeed shal not be, nor his progenie in his people, nor anie remnantes in his countries.
\v 20 In his daie the laſt shal be aſtonied, and horrour shal inuade the firſt.
\v 21 Theſe are then the tabernacles of the wicked man, and this is the place of him that knoweth not God.
\c 19
\cd Iob lamenteth of his freindes crueltie, 6 affirmeth that his ſo great affliction is not for his ſinnes. 25. and comforteth himſelf with his vndoubted beliefe of the Reſurrection.
\p
\v 1 BVT Iob anſwering, ſayd:
\v 2 How long doe you afflict my ſoule, and weare me with wordes?
\v 3 Behold, ten times you counfound me, and are not ashamed oppreſſing me.
\v 4 For although I haue beene ignorant, mine ignorance shal be with me.
\v 5 But you are ſet vp againſt me, and reproue me with my reproches.
\v 6 At the leaſt now vnderſtand ye, that God hath afflicted me \f a \fr 19.6 \ft Bleſſed Iob (ſaith S. Gregorie li. 14. c. 16. 17.) looking ſincerly on his owne life, ſaw that his affliction was greater then his ſinnes deſerued and in that reſpect was not equal: yet it was iuſt, for God, being iuſt geueth a iuſt reward: \+it a crowne of iuſtice\+it* as S. Paul ſpeaketh of himſelf \+it (2. Tim. 4\+it*)\f* not with equal iudgement, and hath compaſſed me with his ſcourges.
\v 7 Behold I shal crie ſuffering violence, and no man wil heare: I shal crie out, and there is none to iudge.
\v 8 He hath hedged my path round about, and I can not paſſe, and in my way hath put darkeneſſe.
\v 9 He hath ſpoiled me of my glorie, and hath taken the crowne from my head.
\v 10 He hath deſtroyed me on euerie ſide, and I perish, and as it were from a tree plucked hath he taken away my hope.
\v 11 His furie is wrath againſt me, and he hath ſo accounted me as his enemie.
\v 12 His ſeriantes haue come together, and haue made them ſelues a way by me, and haue beſieged my tabernacle round about.
\v 13 He hath made my brethren far from me, and my acquaintance as ſtrangers haue departed from me.
\v 14 My kinſemen haue forſaken me, and they that knew me haue forgotten me.
\v 15 The gheſtes of my houſe, and my mayd-ſeruantes haue counted me an aliene, and I haue bene as it were a ſtranger in their eies.
\v 16 I called my ſeruant, and he did not anſwer me, with mine owne mouth I beſought him.
\v 17 My wife hath abhorred my breath, and I prayed the children of my wombe.
\v 18 Fooles alſo deſpiſed me, and when I was departed from them, they backbited me.
\v 19 My counſelers ſometime haue abhorred me: and he \f b \fr 19.19 \ft Al refuſed holie Iob in his affliction euen thoſe whom he had loued moſt: who therfore ought moſt to haue loued him againe.\f* whom I loued moſt hath turned againſt me.
\v 20 The flesh being conſumed my bone hath cleaued to my skinne, and there are left onlie lippes about my teeth.
\v 21 Haue mercie vpon me, haue mercie vpon me, at the leaſt you my frendes, becauſe the hand of our Lord hath touched me.
\v 22 Why doe you as God perſecute me, and are filled with my flesh.
\v 23 Who wil grant me that my wordes may be writen? who wil geue me that they may be drawen in a booke,
\v 24 with yron penne, and in plate of leade, or els with ſtile might be grauen in flintſtone?
\v 25 For \f c \fr 19.25 \ft An expreſſe profeſſion of his faith of the Reſurrection.\f* I know that my Redemer liueth, and in the laſt day I shal riſe out of the earth.
\v 26 And I shal be compaſſed agayne with my skinne, and in my flesh I shal ſee God.
\v 27 Whom I my ſelf shal ſee, and mine eies shal behold, and \f d \fr 19.27 \ft VVe shal riſe againe, not as one tree riſeth in place of an other, buc the ſelf ſame perſones, changed in qualities not in ſubſtance.\f* none other: this my hope is layd vp in my boſome.
\v 28 Why then doe you ſay now: Let vs perſecute him, and let vs finde roote of word agaynſt him?
\v 29 Flee therfore from the face of the ſword, becauſe the ſword is the reuenger of iniquities: and know ye that there is iudgement.
\c 20
\cd Sophar auoucheth that ſome wicked men florish for a time, but are afterwards iuſtly plaged. 19. therupon condemneth Iob as an hypocrite.
\p
\v 1 BVT \f 1 \fr 20.1 \ft The ſixth conflict,\f* Sophar the Naamathite anſwering, ſayd:
\v 2 Therfore my diuerſe cogitations ſuccede one an other, and my minde is rapt into ſundrie things.
\v 3 The doctrine, wherwith thou reproueſt me, I wil heare, and the ſpitit of my vnderſtanding shal anſwer me.
\v 4 This I know from the beginning, ſince man was placed vpon the earth,
\v 5 That the praiſe of the impious is \f a \fr 20.5 \ft Al mans life is short and as a moment in compariſon of eternitie; but it is not alwayes true that the wiked are shortly punished in reſpect of this life, as this diſputer applieth it to proue Iob to impious. Therfore Iob anſwereth in the next chapter. \+it v.13\+it*. That ordinarily the wicked, \+it lead their dayes\+it* (to witt their whole life) \+it in vvealth and\+it* (then) \+it in a moment goe downe to hel\+it*. So the reſt of theſe mens aſſertions are for moſt part true in ſome ſenſe but il applied,\f* short, and the ioy of the hypocrite as it were for a moment:
\v 6 If his pride riſe vp euen to the heauen, and his head touch the cloudes:
\v 7 As a dunghil in the end he shal be deſtroyed, and they that had ſene him, shal ſay: Where is he?
\v 8 As a dreame that fleeth away he shal not qe found, he shal paſſe as a viſion by night:
\v 9 The eie that had ſene him, shal not ſee him, neither shal his place behold him anie more.
\v 10 His children shal come to naught with pouertie, and his handes shal render him his ſorow.
\v 11 His bones shal be filled with the vices of his youth, and they shal ſleepe with him in the duſt.
\v 12 For when euil shal be ſweete in his mouth, he wil hide it vnder his tongue.
\v 13 He wil ſpare it, and not leaue it, and wil hide it in his throte.
\v 14 His bread in his belly shal be turned into the gaule of aſpes within him.
\v 15 The riches, which he hath deuoured, he shal vomite out, and God shal draw them forth out of his belly.
\v 16 He shal ſucke the head of aſpes, and the vipers tongue shal kil him.
\v 17 ( Let him not ſee the ſtreames of the riuer, the torrentes of honie, & of butter.)
\v 18 He shal be punished for al thinges that he did, & yet not be cõſumed: according to the multitude of his inuentions ſo alſo shal he ſuffer.
\v 19 Becauſe breaking in he hath made the poore naked: he hath violently taken houſe, & built it not.
\v 20 Neither is his bellie filled: and when he shal haue the thinges he coueted, he can not poſſeſſe them.
\v 21 There remayned not of his meate, & therfore nothing shal continewe of his goodes:
\v 22 When he shal be filled, he shal be ſtraytened, he shal burne, and al ſorow shal fal vpon him.
\v 23 Would God his belly were filled, that he may ſend forth the wrath of his furie vpon him, and rayne his battel vpon him,
\v 24 He shal flee wepons of yron, and shal fal vpon a bowe of braſſe.
\v 25 The ſword plucked out, and coming forth of his ſcabbard, and gliſtering in his bitterneſſe: the horrible shal goe and come vpon him.
\v 26 Al darkeneſſe is hid in his ſecretes: fyre that is not kindled shal deuoure him, he shal be afflicted leaft in his tabernacle.
\v 27 The heauens shal reuele his iniquitie, and the earth shal riſe againſt him.
\v 28 The bloſſome of his houſe shal be opened, he shal be plucked downe in the day of Gods furie.
\v 29 This the portion of an impions man from God, & the inheritance of his wordes from our Lord.
\c 21
\cd Iob requiring his freindes to heare him, 7. diſcourſeth of the cauſe, why ſome euil men proſper al this life.
\p
\v 1 BVT Iob anſwering, ſayd:
\v 2 Heare I beſech you my wordes, and doe penance.
\v 3 Beare with me, that I alſo may ſpeake, and after my wordes, if it shal ſeme good, laugh ye.
\v 4 Is my diſputation \f a \fr 21.4 \ft Though he diſputed with three men, yet it was concerning diuine thinges not humaine but of Gods prouidence, and iuſtice, of the reſurrection of eternal life and punishment.\f* agaynſt man, that I ought not worthely to be ſorie?
\v 5 Harken to me, and be aſtonied, and put the finger vpon your mouth.
\v 6 And I when I shal remember, am afrayd, and trembling shaketh my flesh.
\v 7 Why then doe the impious liue, are they aduanced, and ſtrengthened with riches?
\v 8 Their ſeede contineweth before them, a multitude of kinſemen, and of nephewes in their ſight.
\v 9 Their houſes be ſecure and peaceable, & the rod of God is not vpon them.
\v 10 Their bullock hath conceiued, and hath not made abortion: their cow hath calued, and is not depriued of her calfe.
\v 11 Their litle ones goe forth as flockes, and their infantes reioyſe with paſtimes.
\v 12 They hold the timbrel, & the harpe, & reioyſe at the ſound of the organe.
\v 13 They \f b \fr 21.13 \ft See \+it ch. 20, v 5\+it*.\f* lead their daies in welth, and in a moment they goe downe to \f c \fr 21.13 \ft The ſame word iſ in Hebrew Greke and Latin for hel as in the \+it 7\+it* and \+it 17 chap\+it*. & other places, which sheweth that beſides hel of the damned the reſting place alſo of holie fathers in the old Teſtament was called by the general name of hel.\f* hel.
\v 14 Who ſayd to God: depart from vs, we wil not the knowlege of thy waies.
\v 15 Who is the Omnipotent, that we should ſerue him? and what doth it profite vs if we shal pray him?
\v 16 But yet becauſe their good things are not in their hand, be the counſel of the impious far from me.
\v 17 How often shal the candel of the impious be extinguished, and inundation come vpon them, and shal he deuide the ſorowes of his furie?
\v 18 They shal be as chaffe before the face of the winde, and as ashes, which the whirlewinde ſcattereth.
\v 19 God shal reſerue the ſorow of the father to his children: and when he shal haue rendred it, then shal he know
\v 20 His eies shal ſee his owne ſlaughter, and he shal drincke the furie of the Omnipotent.
\v 21 For what doeth it pertayne to him concerning his houſe after him: although the number of his monethes be diminished the halfe?
\v 22 Shal anie man teach God knowledge, who iudgeth the high ones.
\v 23 This man dieth ſtrong and in health, rich and happie.
\v 24 His vowels be ful of fatte, and his bones be embrewed with marrow.
\v 25 But an other dieth in bitterneſſe of ſoule without anie riches:
\v 26 And yet they shal ſleepe together in the duſt, and wormes shal couer them.
\v 27 Surely I know your cogitations, and vniuſt ſentences agaynſt me.
\v 28 For you ſay: Where is the houſe of the prince? and where are the tabernacles of the impious?
\v 29 Aſke anie of the wayfaring men, and you shal vnderſtand that he knoweth theſe ſelf ſame thinges.
\v 30 Becauſe the euil man is kept vnto the day of perdition, and he shal be led to the day of furie.
\v 31 Who shal reproue his way before him? and who shal repay him the thinges that he hath done?
\v 32 He shal be brought to the graues, and shal watch in the heade of the dead.
\v 33 He hath beene ſweete to the grauel of \f 1 \fr 21.33 \ft \+it a riuer of hel\+it*.\f* Cocytus, & after him he shal drawe euerie man, and before him innumerable.
\v 34 How therfore doe ye comforth me in vayne, whereas your anſwer is shewed to be repugnant to the truth?
\c 22
\cd Eliphaz contendeth that God is not pleaſed with a iuſt mans afflictions. 5. falſly imputeth enormious crimes to holie Iob, 12. and groſſe errors. 21. Wisheth him therfore to repent, that ſo he may proſper.
\p
\v 1 BVT \f 1 \fr 22.1 \ft The ſeuenth conflict\f* Eliphaz the Themanite anſwering, ſayd:
\v 2 Can man be compared with God, yea though he be of perfect knowlege.
\v 3 What doth it \f a \fr 22.3 \ft In dede whé a iuſt man hath donne his dutie he is vnprofitable to God: but he is profitable to himſelf, which greatly pleaſeth God, who deſireth mans good, and it redoundeth to Gods glorie that he hath ſuch ſeruantes. \+it Mat. 5. v. 17\+it*.\f* profite God if thou be iuſt? or what doeſt thou aduantage him if thy way be vnſpotted.
\v 4 Shal he be afrayde to reproue thee, and come with thee into iudgement:
\v 5 And not for thy very great malice, and thine infinite iniquities?
\v 6 For thou haſt taken away the pledge of thy brethren without cauſe, and the naked thou haſt ſpoyled of clothes.
\v 7 Water to the wearie thou haſt not geuen, and from the hungrie thou haſt withdrawen bread.
\v 8 In the ſtrength of thine arme thou didſt poſſeſſe the earth, and being the mightieſt thou didſt obteyne it.
\v 9 Widowes thou haſt ſent away emptie, and the armes of pupilles thou haſt broken in peeces.
\v 10 Therfore art thou compaſſed with ſnares, and ſoden feare trubleth thee.
\v 11 And thoughteſt thou that thou shouldeſt not ſee darkenes, and that thou shouldeſt not be oppreſſed with the violence of ouerflowing waters?
\v 12 Doeſt thou not thinke that God is higher then heauen, & is exalted aboue the toppe of the ſtarres?
\v 13 And thou ſayeſt: For what knoweth God? and he iudgeth as it were by a miſt.
\v 14 The cloudes are his couert, \f b \fr 22.14 \ft After imputation of falſe crimes, this diſputer chargeth holie Iob alſo with heathnish error of the Ægyptians, that God hath no prouidence of men in this world \+it Ariſtotel. li. de mundo. textu. 84\+it*. So ſome heretikes in their phrenſie accuſe Catholiques of condemned hereſies.\f* neither doth he conſider our thinges, and he walketh about the poles of heauen
\v 15 Doeſt thou couet ro keepe the path of worldes, which wicked men haue troden?
\v 16 Who were taken away before their time, and a floud hath ouerthrowen their foundation:
\v 17 Who ſayd to God: Depart from vs: and as though the Omnipotent could do nothing, they eſtemed him:
\v 18 Whereas he had filled their houſes with good things, whoſe ſentence be far from me.
\v 19 The iuſt shal ſee, and shal reioyſe, and the innocent man shal skorne them
\v 20 Is not their exaltation cut downe, and hath not fire deuoured the remnantes of them?
\v 21 Agree thou therfore to him, and haue peace: and by theſe thinges thou shalt haue the beſt fruites.
\v 22 Receiue the law of his mouth, and put his wordes in thy hart.
\v 23 If thou wilt returne to the Omnipotent, thou shalt be builded vp, and shalt make iniquitie far from thy tabernacle.
\v 24 He shal geue for earth flint, and for flint torrentes of gold.
\v 25 And the Omnipotent shal be agaynſt thine enemies, & ſiluer shal be heaped together vnto thee.
\v 26 Then shalt thou abound in delightes vpon the Omnipoten, and shalt lift vp thy face to God.
\v 27 Thou shalt aske him, and he wil heare thee, and thou shal pay thy vowes.
\v 28 Thou shalt decree a thing, and it shal come to thee, and light shal shine in thy waies.
\v 29 For who wil be humbled, shal be in glorie: and he that wil bow downe his eies, he shal be ſaued.
\v 30 The innocent shal be ſaued, but he shal be ſaued in the cleanneſſe of his handes.
\c 23
\cd Iob expecteth helpe and ſentence of God, 6. with iuſt feare, yet with good conscience maintaineth his owne innocencie.
\p
\v 1 BVT Iob anſwering, ſayd:
\v 2 \f a \fr 23.2 \ft VVhiles he expected ſome comforth of his freindes, they ſtil afflicted him more and more, chargeing him with falſe crimes and ſo aggrauating his grief both of bodie and mind\f* Now alſo my talke is in bitterneſſe, and the hand of my plague is aggrauated vpon my mourning.
\v 3 Who wil grant me that I may know and find him, and come euen to \f b \fr 23.3 \ft Therfore he appealeth to Gods iudgement ſeate for ſentence.\f* his throne?
\v 4 I wil ſet iudgement before him, and wil fil my mouth with accuſations.
\v 5 That I may know the wordes, that he wil anſwer me, and vnderſtand what he wil ſpeake to me.
\v 6 I wil not that he contend with me with much ſtrength, nor that he oppreſſe me with the weight of his greatnes.
\v 7 Let him propoſe equitie againſt me, and my iudgement shal come to victorie.
\v 8 If I shal goe to the Eaſt, he appeareth not: if to the Weſt, I shal not vnderſtand him.
\v 9 If to the left band, what shal I doe? I shal not apprehend him: if I turne my ſelf to the right hand, I shal not ſee him.
\v 10 But he knoweth my way, & hath proued me as gold that paſſeth through the fyre:
\v 11 My foote hath folowed his ſteppes, I haue kept his way, & haue not declined out if it.
\v 12 From the commandementes of his lippes I haue not departed, and I haue hid the wordes of his mouth in my boſome.
\v 13 For he is alone, and no man can turne away his cogitation: and whatſoeuer his ſoule would, that hath he done.
\v 14 And when he shal haue fulfilled his wil in me, manie other things alſo are at hand wit him.
\v 15 And therfore I am trubled at his face, and conſidering, him I am made penſife withfeare.
\v 16 God hath mollified my hart, and the Omnipotent hath trubled me.
\v 17 For I haue not perished becauſe of the imminent darkeneſſe, neither hath the miſt couered my face.
\c 24
\cd God in his prouidence knoweth when he wil punish the wicked, which his true ſeruantes know not, much leſſe the impious.
\p
\v 1 TIMES are not hid from the Omnipotent: but they \f a \fr 24.1 \ft Gods ſeruantes know that he wil puniſh wickednes, but know not when: the wicked preſume that he wil neuer puniſh them.\f* that know him, know not his daies.
\v 2 Some haue transferred boundes, ſpoiled flockes, & fed them.
\v 3 They haue driuen away the aſſe of pupilles, & haue taken away the widowes oxe for a pledge.
\v 4 They haue ſubuerted the way of the poore, and haue oppreſſed together the meeke of the earth.
\v 5 Others as wilde aſſes in the deſerte goe forth to their worke: watching to the praye, doe prepare bread for their children.
\v 6 They reape the filde that is not theirs, and gather the grapes of his vineyard, whom by violence they haue oppreſſed.
\v 7 They ſend men away naked, taking away their clothes, which haue no couering in the cold.
\v 8 Whom the ſhowers of the mountaynes doe wash, and not hauing a couert, they embrace ſtones.
\v 9 They did violence ſpoyling the pupilles, and the common poore people they ſpoyled.
\v 10 From the naked and them that goe without clothing, and the hungrie they haue taken away the eares of corne.
\v 11 They haue reſted the noonetide among their heapes, which hauing troden the wine preſſes are a thirſt.
\v 12 Out of the cities they haue made men to mourne, and the ſoule of the wounded hath cryed, and God doth not ſuffer it to paſſe vnreuenged:
\v 13 They haue bene \f b \fr 24.13 \ft Heretikes doing and teaching againſt their owne knowlege, are afterward ſtriken with blindnes. that they can not ſee the truth. S. Greg. li. 16. c. 26.\f* rebellious to the light, they haue not knowen his wayes, neither did they returne by his pathes.
\v 14 At the verie breake of day the murderer ryſeth, he killeth the needie, and the poore man: but by night he wil be as a theefe.
\v 15 The eie of the aduouterer obſerueth darkeneſſe, ſaying: Eie shal not ſee me: and he wil couer his face.
\v 16 He diggeth through houſes in the darke, as in the day they had oppoynted with them ſelues, and they haue not knowen the light.
\v 17 If ſodenly the morning shal appeare, they thinke it the ſhadow of death: and they walke ſo in darkeneſſe as it were in light.
\v 18 He is light aboue the face of the water: curſed be his portion in the earth, neither walke he by the way of the vineyardes.
\v 19 Let him paſſe \f c \fr 24.19 \ft Sinners running into both extremes of defect and exceſſe, are likewiſe puniſhed with contrarie tormentes.\f* from ſnowe waters to exceding heate, and his ſinne euen vnto hel.
\v 20 Let mercie forget him: wormes his ſweetnes: be he not in remembrance, but be he broken in peeces as an vnfruitful tree.
\v 21 For he hath fedd the barren, and her that bareth not, and to the widow he hath not done good.
\v 22 He hath pulled downe the ſtrong in his ſtrength, and when he shal ſtand, he wil not credit his life.
\v 23 God hath geuen him place for penance, and he \f d \fr 24.23 \ft Man by powre of free wil oftẽ preſumeth to ſpend the time in ſinning which God granteth him to do penance, for former ſinnes. \+it Rom. 2\+it*.\f* abuſeth it vnto pride: but his eies be vpon his waies.
\v 24 They are eleuated for a litle, and shal not ſtand, and shal be humbled as al thinges, and shal be taken away, and as the toppes of the eares of corne they shal be broken.
\v 25 And if it be not ſo, who can reproue me that I haue lied, and ſet my wordes before God?
\c 25
\cd Baldad endeuoreth againe to terrifie Iob, with Gods iudgement, from appealing therto, and from auouching his owne innocencie.
\p
\v 1 BVT \f 1 \fr 25.1 \ft The eighth conflict.\f* Baldad the Suhite anſwering, ſayd:
\v 2 Power and terrour is with him, that maketh concord in his high ones.
\v 3 Is there anie number of his ſouldiars? and vpon whom shal not his light ariſe,
\v 4 \f a \fr 25.4 \ft Iob anſwered before. \+it ch. 9\+it*. yet blind and obſtinate diſputers ſtil repete the ſame obiections.\f* can man be iuſtified compared with God, or the borne of a woman appeare cleane?
\v 5 Behold the moone alſo doth not ſhine, and the ſtarres are not cleane in his ſight.
\v 6 How much more man rottennes, & the ſome of man a worme?
\c 26
\p
\v 1 BVT Iob anſwering, ſayd:
\v 2 \f a \fr 26.2 \ft Iob sheweth that neither God nedeth mans helpe:\f* Whoſe helper art thou? his that is weake? and doeſt thou hold vp the arme of him, that is not ſtrong?
\v 3 To whom haſt thou geuen counſel? perhaps to him, that hath not wiſdom, and \f b \fr 26.3 \ft Neither is Baldads prudence able to helpe if there were nede:\f* thy prudence haſt thou ſhewed very great.
\v 4 Whom wouldeſt thou teach? not him that made breath?
\v 5 Behold the \f c \fr 26.5 \ft Giantes were not able to wade in Noes floud, but were drowned with the reſt.\f* gyantes grone vnder the waters, and they that dwel with them.
\v 6 Hel is naked before him, and there is no couert to perdition.
\v 7 Who ſtretcheth out the northwind ouer the vacant, and hangeth the earth vpon nothing.
\v 8 Who bindeth the waters in his cloudes, that they breake not forth together downeward.
\v 9 Who holdeth the countenance of his throne, and ſpreddeth his clowde ouet it.
\v 10 He hath made a limite about the waters, til light & darkenes be ended.
\v 11 The pillers of heauen tremble, and dread at his beck.
\v 12 In his ſtrength ſodenly the ſeas are gathered together, and with his wiſdom he ſtroke the proud man.
\v 13 His ſpirite hath adorned the heauens, and his hand being the midwife, the \f d \fr 26.13 \ft Not only great thinges before recited but alſo the very leaſt are made by God, and depend vpon his prouidence.\f* winding ſerpent is brought forth.
\v 14 Loe, theſe things are ſayd in part of his waies: and where as we haue heard ſcarce a litle droppe of his word, who shal be able to behold the thunder of his greatnes?
\c 27
\cd More and more Iob confirmeth his innocencie, auouching that God not preſently iudging; 11. Wil in time condemne the wicked.
\p
\v 1 IOB alſo added, taking his parable, and ſayd:
\v 2 God liueth, who hath \f a \fr 27.2 \ft God would not as yet haue Iobs cauſe iudged, but reſerued the ſentence, for his greater trial in patience.\f* taken away my iudgement, and the Omnipotent, which hath brought my ſoule to bitterneſſe.
\v 3 That as long as breath remaineth in me, and the ſpirit of God in my noſthrels,
\v 4 My lippes shal not ſpeake iniquitie, neither shal my tongue meditate \f b \fr 27.4 \ft It were a lie to acknowlege ſuch ſinnes as he had not committed.\f* lying.
\v 5 God forbid that I ſhould iudge you to be iuſt: til I faile, I wil not departe from mine innocencie.
\v 6 My iuſtification which I haue begune to hold, I wil not forſake: for my hart doth not reprehend me in al my life.
\v 7 Let mine enemie be as the impions, and mine aduerſarie as the wicked one.
\v 8 For what is the hypocrites hope if couetouſely he take by violence, and God deliuer not his ſoule?
\v 9 Wil God heare his crie, when diſtreſſe shal come vpon him?
\v 10 Or can he be delighted in the Omnipotent, and inuocate God at al time?
\v 11 I wil teach you by the hand of God, what the Omnipotent hath, neither wil I hide it.
\v 12 Loe, you doe al knowe, and why ſpeake you vaine thinges without cauſe.
\v 13 This is the portion of an impions man with God, and inheritance of the violent, which they shal receiue of the Omnipotent.
\v 14 If his children be multiplied, they shal be in the ſword, & nephewes shal not be filled with bread.
\v 15 They that shal be leaft of him, shal be buried in death, and his widowes shal not weepe.
\v 16 If he shal heape together ſiluer as earth, and as clay shal prepare garmentes:
\v 17 He shal prepare in deede, but the iuſt man shal be clothed with them: and the innocent shal diuide the ſiluer.
\v 18 He hath built his houſe as a moth, and as a keeper hath he made a bowre.
\v 19 The rich man \f c \fr 27.19 \ft Some part of Gods iudgement falleth on the wicked in this life, but eſpecially at their death. \+it Pſal. 75\+it*.\f* when he shal ſleepe shal take away nothing with him: he shal oppen his eies, & finde nothing.
\v 20 Pouertie as water shal apprehend him, in the night a tempeſt shal oppreſſe him.
\v 21 The burning wind shal take him vp, and cary him away, and as a whirlewinde shal pul him violently out of his place.
\v 22 And he shal caſt vpon him, and shal not ſpare: out of his hand fleing he shal flee.
\v 23 He shal claſpe his handes vpon him, and shal hiſſe vpon him, beholding his place.
\c 28
\cd The maruelous workes of God, the author of nature, shew his powre and wiſdome, 12. and that true riches conſiſt not in temporal creatures, but in wiſdome, 28. and feare of God.
\p
\v 1 SILVER hath \f a \fr 28.1 \ft By theſe more precious and rare creatures, men ought to conſider the creator and ſo not ſet their reſt in them, but in him, which is true wiſdome.\f* beginnings of her vaines, and gold hath a place, where in it is molten.
\v 2 Yron is taken out of the earth, and ſtone reſolued with heate, is turned into braſſe.
\v 3 He hath ſet a time for darkeneſſe, and the end of al thinges he conſidereth, the ſtone alſo of darkeneſſe, and the shadow of death.
\v 4 The \f b \fr 28.4 \ft Sudaine headie waters burſting out do change the wayes and paſſages of men.\f* torrent diuideth from the pilgrime people, them whom the foote of the needie man hath forgotten, and to whom there is no way.
\v 5 The land, out of which bread grew in his place, is deſtroyed with fire.
\v 6 The place of the ſapphire the ſtones thereof, and the cloddes of it gold.
\v 7 The bird hath not knowen the path, neither hath the eie of the vulter beheld it.
\v 8 The children of merchantes haue not troden it, neither hath the lioneſſe paſſed by it.
\v 9 He hath ſtretched forth his hand to the flint, he hath ouerthrowen mountaines from the rootes.
\v 10 In the rockes he hath cut out riuers, and his eie hath ſeene euerie pretious thing.
\v 11 The depthes alſo of riuers he hath ſearched, & hid things he hath brought forth to light:
\v 12 But where is wiſdome to be found, and what is the place of vnderſtanding?
\v 13 Man knoweth not the price therof, \f c \fr 28.13 \ft True wiſdom is not in natural thinges, but in ſupernatural vertues.\f* neither is it found in the land of them that liue pleaſently.
\v 14 The depth ſayth: It is not in me: and the ſea ſpeaketh: It is not with me.
\v 15 The fineſt gold shal not be geuen for it, neither ſhal ſiluer be weyed in the change therof.
\v 16 It shal not be compared with the died colours of India, nor with Sardonyx the moſt pretious ſtone, or with the Sapphire.
\v 17 Gold or glaſſe shal not be equal to it, neither ſhal veſſels of gold be changed for it.
\v 18 High and eminent thinges shal not be mentioned in compariſon of it: and wiſedom is drawen out of ſecrete places.
\v 19 The topazius of Æthiopia shal not be equal to it, neither shal it be compared to the cleaneſt diyng.
\v 20 From whence then cometh wiſedom? and what is the place of vnderſtanding?
\v 21 It is hid from the eies of al liuing, the foules of the ayre alſo know it not.
\v 22 Perdition and death haue ſayd: With our eares we haue heard the fame therof.
\v 23 God vnderſtandeth the way of it, and he knoweth the place therof.
\v 24 For he beholdeth the endes of the world: & looketh on al thinges that are vnder heauen.
\v 25 Who made a poiſe to the windes, and weyed the waters in a meaſure.
\v 26 When he gaue a lawe to the raynes, and a way to the ſounding ſtormes.
\v 27 Then he ſaw it, and declared, and prepared and ſearched it.
\v 28 And he ſayd to man: Behold \f d \fr 28.28 \ft VVhen man hath conſidered Gods workes, his dutie is to feare God\f* the feare of our Lord, that is wiſedom: and to \f e \fr 28.28 \ft Then to depart from euil and do good.\f* depart from euil vnderſtanding.
\c 29
\cd Againe Iob recounteth Gods former benefites, as wel his grace, wherby he did good workes, 5. as temporal proſperitie. 9. and wiſdome aboue other princes.
\p
\v 1 IOB alſo added, taking \f a \fr 29.1 \ft Parables are not only ſimilitudes of thinges but alſo pithie and profound ſentences ſuch as Iob, Salomon, and other wiſeſt men vttered.\f* his parable, and ſayd:
\v 2 Who wil grant me, that I may be according to the former monethes, according to the daies in which God kept me?
\v 3 When his lampe ſhined ouer my head, & I walked by his light in darknes?
\v 4 As I was in the daies of my youth, when God was ſecretly in my tabernacle?
\v 5 When the Omnipotent was with me: and my ſeruantes round about me?
\v 6 When I washed my feete with butter, and the rocke powred me riuers of oile?
\v 7 When I went forth to the gatte of the citie, and in the ſtreate they prepared me a chaire?
\v 8 Yong men ſawe me, and hid them ſelues: and old men riſing vp ſtoode.
\v 9 The princes ceaſed to ſpeake, and did put the finger vpon their mouth.
\v 10 Dukes held in their voice, and their tongue cleaued to their throte.
\v 11 The eare hearing counted me bleſſed, and the eie ſeing gaue teſtimonie to me:
\v 12 For that I had deliuered the poore man crying out, and the pupil, that had no helper.
\v 13 The bleſſing of him that was readie to perish came vpon me, and I conforted the hart of the widow.
\v 14 I was clothed with iuſtice: and I reueſted me with my iudgement, as with a garment and crowne.
\v 15 I was an eie to the blind, and a foote to the lame.
\v 16 I was the father of the poore: and the cauſe which I knew not, I ſearched moſt diligently.
\v 17 I brake the iawes of the wicked man, and out of his teeth I tooke away the praye.
\v 18 And I ſaid: I wil die in my litle neſt, & as a palme-tree wil multiplie daies.
\v 19 My roote is opened beſide the waters, and dewe shal continue in my harueſt.
\v 20 My glorie shal alwaies be renewed, and my bow in my hand shal be repayred.
\v 21 They that heard me, expected ſentence, and attent held their peace at my counſel.
\v 22 To my wordes they durſt adde nothing, and my ſpeach diſtilled vpon them.
\v 23 They expected me as rayne, and they opened their mouth as it were to a lateward shower.
\v 24 If at anie time I laughed on them, they beleued not, and the light of my countenance fel not on the earth.
\v 25 If I would haue gone to them, I ſate firſt, and when I ſate \f b \fr 29.25 \ft This particle (as) importeth not here a ſimilitude but rather that he was a very king or ſupreme prince as hauing ſupreme authoritie. \+it v.7\+it*. royal veſture and crowne \+it v.14. Iſidorus, li. de vita Sanct. Beda & alij\+it*.\f* as a king, with his armie ſtanding about him, yet was I a conforter of them that mourned.
\c 30
\cd Holie Iob sheweth the great change of his temporal eſtate, from welfare into great calamitie.
\p
\v 1 BVT now they of yonger time ſcorne me, whoſe fathers I vouchſafed not to put \f a \fr 30.1 \ft Men ſcarſe fitte to haue care of dogges derided Iob in his affliction ſo he was contemned of the moſt contemptible.\f* with the dogs of my flocke:
\v 2 The force of whoſe handes was to me as nothing, and they were thought vnworthie of life it ſelf.
\v 3 Barren with pouertie and famine, who gnawed in the wildernes, il fauoured by calamitie and miſerie.
\v 4 And they did eate graſſe, and the barkes of trees, and the roote of iunipers was their meat.
\v 5 Who taking theſe thinges violently out of the valles, when they had found euerie thing, they ranne to them with a crie.
\v 6 They dwelt in the deſerts of torrentes, and in caues of the earth, or vpon grauel.
\v 7 Who reioyſed among theſe kind of thinges, and counted it delicacies to be vnder the briars.
\v 8 The children of foolish and baſe men, and in the earth not appearing at al.
\v 9 Now am I turned into their ſong, and become a prouerb with them.
\v 10 They abhorre me, and flee far from me, and are not a frayd \f b \fr 30.10 \ft Our Sauiour alſo ſuffered this reproch. \+it Mat. 26.\+it* · In the Hebrew \+it haue put\+it*, in the plural number, importing pluralitie of Diuine Perſons.\f* to ſpit in my face.
\v 11 For he hath opened his quiuer, and hath afflicted me, and hath put a bridle into my mouth.
\v 12 At the right hand of me riſing, my calamities forthwith aroſe: they haue ouerthrowen my feete, and as with waues haue oppreſſed with their pathes.
\v 13 They haue diſſipated my waies, they haue lyen in wayte againſt me, and they haue preuailed, and there was not that would helpe.
\v 14 As when a wal is broken, and the gate opened, they haue broken violently vpon me, and are come trambling downe to my miſeries.
\v 15 I brought to nothing as a wind he hath taken away my deſire mand my proſperitie hath paſſed away as a clowde.
\v 16 And now my ſoule withereth in my ſelf, and the daies of affliction poſſeſſe me.
\v 17 In the night my bone is pearſed with ſorrowes: and they that eate me, ſleepe not.
\v 18 In the multitude of them my garment is conſumed, and they haue girded me about, as it were with the coler of a wate.
\v 19 I am compared to durt, and am reſembled to imbers and ashes.
\v 20 I crie to thee, and thou heareſt me not: I ſtand, and thou doeſt not reſpect me.
\v 21 Thou art changed to be cruel roward me, and in the hardeneſſe of thy hand thou art againſt me.
\v 22 Thou didſt lift me vp, and ſetting me as it were vpon the wind thou haſt mightely dashed me.
\v 23 I know \f c \fr 30.23 \ft Death is a comforth to a iuſt man in tribulation.\f* that thou wilt deliuer me to death, where a houſe is appointed for euery one that liueth.
\v 24 But yet not to my conſumption doeſt thou ſend forth thy hand: and if they shal fal, thou wilt ſaue.
\v 25 I wept ſometime vpon him that was afflicted, and my ſoul had compaſſion on the poore.
\v 26 I expect good thinges, and euils are come vpon me: I taried for light, and darkeneſſe brake forth.
\v 27 My inner partes haue boyled without anie reſt, the dayes of affliction haue preuented me.
\v 28 I went mourning without furie, riſing vp, I cried in the multitude.
\v 29 I was the \f d \fr 30.29 \ft Like to ſuch beaſtes as ſeke ſolitary places to lament in.\f* brother of dragons, and felow of Oſtriches.
\v 30 My skinne is made blacke vpon me, and my bones are dried with heate.
\v 31 My harpe is turned into mourning, and my inſtrument into the voice of weepers.
\c 31
\cd Holie Iob reciteth ſincerly his owne vertues, shewing therby that he is not punished ſo grieuouſly for his ſinnes, but by Gods prouidence for ſome other cauſe.
\p
\v 1 I HAVE \f a \fr 31.1 \ft wheras there is a continual warre betwen a chaiſt mind and rebellious flesh, holie Iob made this condition of truce betwene theſe enimies: that his eye ſhould neuer geue occaſion to carnal concupiſcence.\f* made a couenant with mine eyes, that I would not ſo much as \f b \fr 31.1 \ft By which meanes, he was alſo ſafe from carnal cogitations. \+it S. Greg. li. 21. e, 2\+it*.\f* thinke of a virgin.
\v 2 For what part ſhould God from aboue haue in me, & inheritance the Omnipotent from on high?
\v 3 Is there not perdition to the wicked man, and alienation to them that worke iniuſtice?
\v 4 Doth not he conſider my waies, and number al my ſteppes?
\v 5 If I haue walked in vanitie, and my foote hath haſtened in guile:
\v 6 Let him wey me in a iuſt balance, and let God know my ſimplicitie.
\v 7 If my ſteppe haue declined out of the way, and if mine eie hath folowed my hart, and if ſpotte hath cleaued to my handes:
\v 8 Let me ſawe, and let an other eate it: and let my progenie be plucked vp by the rootes.
\v 9 If my hart hath bene deceiued vpon a woman, and if I haue lyene in waite at my freinds doore:
\v 10 Let my wife be the harlot of an other man, and let other men lye with her.
\v 11 For this is a hainous thing, and moſt great iniquitie.
\v 12 It is a fire deuoring euen to perdition, and rooting vp al thinges that ſpring.
\v 13 If I hane contemned to abide iudgement with my man ſeruant, and my mayd ſeruant, when they had anie controuerſie againſt me.
\v 14 For what ſhal I doe when God shal riſe to iudge? and when he shal aske, what shal I anſwer him?
\v 15 Did not he make me in the wombe that made him alſo: and did not one forme me in the matrice?
\v 16 If I haue denied to the poore, that which they would, and haue made the eyes of the widow to expect:
\v 17 If I haue eaten my morſel alone, and the pulpil hath not eaten therof with me. (
\v 18 Becauſe from mine infancie mercy hath growen with me: and from my mothers wombe it came forth with me. )
\v 19 If I haue diſpiſed him that perisheth, for that he had not clothing, and the poore man without wherwithal to couer him:
\v 20 If his ſides haue not bleſſed me, & he was not warmed with the fliſes of my sheepe:
\v 21 If I haue lifted vp my hand ouer the pupil, yea when I ſaw my ſelf in the gate the ſuperior:
\v 22 Let my ſhoulder fal from his iuncture, and let my arme with his bones be broken.
\v 23 For I haue alwaies feared God as waues ſwelling vpon me, and his weight I could not beare.
\v 24 If I haue thought gold my ſtrength, and haue ſaid to fine gold: My confidence.
\v 25 If I haue reioyſed vpon my great riches, and becauſe my hand found manie thinges.
\v 26 If I ſaw the ſunne when it shined, and the moone going cleerly:
\v 27 And my hart in ſecret reioyſed, and I kiſſed my hand with my mouth.
\v 28 Which is \f c \fr 31.28 \ft By this demand he prouoked his aduerſaries to ptoduce what they could to conuince him of idolatrie or denying God wherwith they indirectly charged him,\f* moſt great iniquitie, and a denial againſt God the moſt high?
\v 29 If I haue beene glad of his fal, that hated me, & haue reioyſed that euil had found him.
\v 30 For I haue not geuen my throte to ſinne, that curſing I wished his ſoule.
\v 31 If the men of my tabernacle haue not ſayd: Who wil giue of his flesh that we may be filled?
\v 32 The ſtranger taried not without, my doore was open to the wayfaring man
\v 33 If as man I haue hid my ſinne, and haue concealed my iniquitie in my boſome.
\v 34 If I haue bene afrayd at a verie great multitude, & the contempt of kinſmen hath terrified me: and I haue not rather held my peace, & not gone out of the doore.
\v 35 Who wil grant me an hearer, that the Omnipotent would heare my deſire: and that himſelf that iudgeth would write a booke.
\v 36 That I may carie it on my ſhoulder, and put it about me as a crowne?
\v 37 At euerie ſteppe of mine I wil pronounce it, and as to the prince I wil offer it.
\v 38 If my Land cry againſt me, and with it the furrowes therof lament:
\v 39 If I haue eaten the fruites therof without money, and haue afflicted the ſoule of the tillers therof.
\v 40 For wheate let the bryar grow to me, and for barlie the thorne. The wordes of Iob are ended. \f d \fr 31.40 \ft Iob diſputed no more with his freindes: but afterward ſubmitted him ſelf to God, acknowledging ſome vnaduiſed ſpeach. \+it c. 39. v. 37. c. 42. v. 3\+it*.(d) \f*
\c 32
\cd Eliu a young man being angrie that Iob perſiſted in his opinion, and that his three freindes could not conuince him, 8. taketh vpon him to confute Iob which they could not do.
\p
\v 1 BVT \f 1 \fr 32.1 \ft The ninth conflict.\f* theſe three men omitted to anſwer Iob, for that he ſeemed iuſt to himſelf.
\v 2 And \f a \fr 32.2 \ft This young-man wittie & lerned, but proud withal, was a figure of the hote and arrogant diſputers who wil ſeme to know more then their elders. \+it S. Greg. li. 23. c. 2\+it*.\f* Eliu the ſonne of Barachel a Buzite, of the kinred of Ram, was angrie and tooke indignation: and he was angrie againſt Iob, for that he ſayd himſelf to be iuſt before God.
\v 3 Moreouer againſt his freindes he had indignation, for that they had not found a reaſonable anſwer, but onlie had condemned Iob.
\v 4 Therfore Eliu expected Iob ſpeaking, becauſe they were his elders that ſpake.
\v 5 But when he ſaw that the three were not able to anſwer, he was wrath excedingly.
\v 6 And Eliu the ſonne of Barachel a Buzite anſwering, ſayd: I am yonger in time, and you more ancient, therfore caſting downe my head, I was afrayd to shewe yon my ſentence.
\v 7 For I hoped that longer age would ſpeake, and that a multitnde of yeares would teach wiſdom.
\v 8 But as I ſee, there is a Spirite in men, and the inſpiration of the Omnipotent geueth vnderſtanding.
\v 9 They of many yeares are not the wiſe men, neither doe the ancientes vnderſtand iudgement.
\v 10 Therfore wil I ſpeake: Heare ye me, I alſo wil shew you my wiſedom.
\v 11 For I haue expected your wordes, I haue heard your wiſdom, as long as you contended in wordes.
\v 12 And as long as I thought you ſaid ſomewhat, I conſidered: but as I ſee, \f b \fr 32.12 \ft A notorious arrogancie to hold himſelfe wiſer then any man, either of his owne ſect, or of his aduerſaries.\f* here is none of you that can reproue Iob, and anſwer to his wordes.
\v 13 Leſt perhaps you may ſay: We haue found wiſedom, God hath reiected, him not man.
\v 14 He hath ſpoken nothing \f c \fr 32.14 \ft Thoſe that neither credite Catholique Doctors, nor yet relie vpon their owne elders, but euery one vpon his owne priuate ſpirit, denie that to perteyne to them which is ſpoken to others in the ſame errors. \+it S. Greg. li. 23. c. 8\+it*.\f* to me, and I wil not anſwer him according to your wordes.
\v 15 They were afrayd, and anſwered no more, & they haue taken away talke from themſelues.
\v 16 Therfore becauſe I haue expected, and they ſpake not: they ſtoode, & anſwered no more:
\v 17 I alſo wil anſwer my part, and wil shew my knowledge.
\v 18 For I am ful of wordes, and the ſpirit of my bellie ſtreyneth me.
\v 19 Behold, my bellie is as new wine without a vent, which breaketh new veſſels.
\v 20 I wil ſpeake, and take breath a litle: I wil open my lippes, and wil anſwer.
\v 21 I wil not accept the perſon of a man, and I wil not make God equal to man.
\v 22 For I know not how long I shal continewe, and whether after a while my maker wil take me away.
\c 33
\cd Eliu endeuoreth to proue by Iobs ſpeach that he is vniuſt: 13. arguing that God (by afflicting him) hath alreadly ſo iudged. 23. but if by an Angels admonition he repent, al shal be remitted.
\p
\v 1 HEERE therfore Iob my ſayings, and harken to al my wordes.
\v 2 Behold I haue opened my mouth, let my tongue ſpeake within my iawes.
\v 3 My wordes are of my ſimple hart, and my lippes shal ſpeake a pure ſentence.
\v 4 The Spirit of God made me, and the breath of the Omnipotent gaue me life.
\v 5 If thou canſt, anſwer me, and ſtand againſt my face.
\v 6 Behold God hath made me alſo euen as thee, and of the ſame clay I alſo was formed.
\v 7 But yet let not my \f a \fr 33.7 \ft Arrogant men imagine their owne conceiptes, & vtterance to be more meruelous then other mens, not knowing how foolish their owne pride is. \+it S. Greg. li. 23. c. 16\+it*.\f* miracle terrifie thee, and let not my eloquence be burdenous to thee.
\v 8 Thou therfore haſt ſayd in my eares, and I haue heard the voice of thy wordes:
\v 9 I am cleane, and without ſinne: vnſpotted, and there is no iniquitie in me.
\v 10 Becauſe he hath found quarrels in me, therfore hath he thought me his enemy
\v 11 He hath put my feete in the ſtockes, he hath obſerued al my waies.
\v 12 This therfore is it, wherein thou art not iuſtified: I wil anſwer thee, that God is greater then man.
\v 13 Doeſt thou contend againſt him becauſe he hath not anſwered thee to al wordes?
\v 14 God \f b \fr 33.14 \ft It is true that Gods wil once vttered ought to ſuffice al men, for he anſwereth not to ech one by a particular ſpeach, but by common ſpeach (or fact) ſatisfieth al mens queſtions. wherof S. Gregorie noteth this general rule. \+it vita præcedentium fit forma ſequentium. The life of them that goe before is made a forme\+it* (or rule) \+it of them that folovv. li. 23. c. 18 & 19\+it*. But Eliu falſely ſuppoſed that God by Iobs affliction had declared that he was a wicked man. For in dede God declared the contrarie \+it c. 1. v. 1. & 8. c. 2. v. 3\+it*.\f* ſpeaketh once, & repeateth not the ſelf ſame the ſecond time.
\v 15 By a dreame in a viſion by night, when heauie ſleepe falleth vpon men, and they ſleepe in their bed.
\v 16 Thē doth he open the eares of men, & teaching inſtructeth them with diſcipline,
\v 17 That he may turne a man from theſe things, which he doth, & may deliuer him from pride:
\v 18 Deliuering his ſoule from corruption: and his life, that it paſſe not vnto the ſword.
\v 19 He rebuketh alſo by ſorow in the bed, and he maketh al his bones to wither.
\v 20 Bread is become abominable to him in his life, and to his ſoule the meate before deſired.
\v 21 His flesh ſhal conſume, and the bones that had beene couered, shal be made naked.
\v 22 His ſoule hath approched to corruption, and his life to things cauſing death.
\v 23 If there shal be an Angel ſpeaking for him, one of thouſandes, to declare mans equitie.
\v 24 He shal haue mercie on him, and shal ſay: deliuer him, that he deſcend not into corruption: I haue found wherein I may be propitious to him.
\v 25 His fleſh is conſumed with punishments, let it returne to the daies of his youth.
\v 26 He shal beſeche God, and he wil be pacified towards him: and he shal ſee his face in iubilation, and he wil render to a man his iuſtice.
\v 27 He shal behold men, and shal ſay: I haue ſinned, and in deede I haue offended, and, as I was worthie, I haue not receiued.
\v 28 He hath deliuered his ſoule that it ſhould not goe into death, but liuing ſhould ſee the light.
\v 29 Behold, al theſe things doth God worke three times in euerie one.
\v 30 That he may reclame their ſoules from corruption, and illuminate them with the light of the liuing.
\v 31 Attend Iob, and heare me: and hold thy peace, whiles I ſpeake.
\v 32 But if thou haſt what to ſpeake, anſwer me, ſpeake: for I would thee to appeare iuſt.
\v 33 And if thou haue not, heare me: hold thy peace, and I wil teach thee wiſedom.
\c 34
\cd Againe Eliu chargeth Iob with blaſphemie, and other crimes, 10. sheweth the equitie of Gods iudgement: 20. and that al thinges are ſubiect to Gods powre and knowlege.
\p
\v 1 ELIV therfore pronouncing, ſpake theſe wordes alſo.
\v 2 Heare ye wiſe men my wordes, and ye learned harken to me:
\v 3 For the eare proueth wordes, and the throate diſcerneth meates by the taſt.
\v 4 Let vs chooſe vs iudgement, and among vs let vs ſee what is the better.
\v 5 Becauſe Iob ſayd: I am iuſt, and God hath \f a \fr 34.5 \ft Iob ſaid not that God \+it ſubuerted iudgement\+it* (or wrongfully iudged) but God \+it hath taken away my iudgement. cha. 27 v. 2\+it*. that is, differred to iudge my cauſe.\f* ſubuerted my iudgement.
\v 6 For in iudging me there is a lie: mine arrow is violent without anie ſinne.
\v 7 What man is there as is Iob, that drinketh ſkorning as it were water?
\v 8 That goeth with them that worke iniquitie, and walketh with impious men?
\v 9 For he hath ſayd: Man shal not pleaſe God \f b \fr 34.9 \ft Neither did Iob ſay this \+it (c. 9. v. 22\+it*.) but Eliu wreſted his meaning that he might haue ſomething to reprehend \+it S. Greg. li. 24. c. 25\+it*.\f* although he runne with him.
\v 10 Therfore ye diſcrete men heare me, far from God be impietie, and iniquitie from the Omnipotent.
\v 11 For he wil render a man his worke, and according to the waies of euerie one he wil recompence them.
\v 12 For in verie deed God wil not condemne without cauſe, neither wil the Omnipotent ſubuert iudgement.
\v 13 What other hath he appointed ouer the earth? or whom hath he ſette ouer the world, which he made?
\v 14 If he direct his hart to him, he shal drawe his ſpirit and breath vnto him.
\v 15 Al flesh shal faile together, and man shal returne into ashes.
\v 16 If then thou haue vnderſtanding, heare that is ſayd, and harken to the voice of my ſpeach.
\v 17 Can he that loueth not iudgement, be healed? and how doeſt thou ſo far condemne him, that is iuſt?
\v 18 That ſayth to the king, Apoſtata: that calleth dukes impious:
\v 19 Who accepteth not the perſones of princes: nor hath knowen the tyrant, when he contended againſt the poore man: for al are the worke of his handes.
\v 20 They shal ſodenly die, and at midnight peoples shal be trubled, and shal paſſe, and take away the violent without hand.
\v 21 For his eies are vpon the waies of men, and he conſidereth al their ſteppes.
\v 22 There is not darkeneſſe, and there is not shadow of death, that they may be hid there which worke iniquitie.
\v 23 For it is no more in mans powre, to come to God into iudgement.
\v 24 He shal deſtroy manie, & innumerable, & shal make other to ſtand for them.
\v 25 For he knoweth their workes: and therfore he shal bring night, and they shal be deſtroyed.
\v 26 As impious men he hath ſtroken them in the place of them that ſee.
\v 27 Who as it were of purpoſe haue reuolted from him, and would not vnderſtand al his waies:
\v 28 That they cauſed the crie of the needie man to come to him, and he heard the voice of the poore.
\v 29 For he granting peace, who is there that can condemne? After he shal hide his countenance, who is there that may behold him, both vpon nations, and vpon al men?
\v 30 Who maketh a man that is \f b \fr 34.30 \ft Eliu applieth this to Iob, as though he had made falſe shew of vertue which he had not, and that God had ſuffered him hitherto to reigne, or rather to tyrannize for iuſt punishment of his peoples ſinnes. But God at laſt declared that Iob was not ſuch a one. ch. 42. v. 7.\f* an hypocrite reigne for the ſinnes of the people.
\v 31 Therfore becauſe I haue ſpoken to God, thee alſo I wil not prohibite.
\v 32 If I haue erred, teach thou me: if I haue ſpoken iniquitie, I wil adde no more.
\v 33 Doth God require it of thee, becauſe it hath diſpleaſed thee? for thou beganſt to ſpeake, and not I: but if thou know anie better thing, ſpeake.
\v 34 Let men of vnderſtanding ſpeake to me, and let a wiſeman heare me.
\v 35 But Iob hath ſpoken folishly, and his wordes ſound not diſcipline.
\v 36 O my father, let Iob be proued euen to the end: ceaſe not from the man of iniquity.
\v 37 Who addeth blaſphemie vpon his ſinnes, let him be reſtrayned in the meane time among vs: and then let him prouoke God to iudgement with his ſpeaches.
\c 35
\cd Eliu pretending that Iob had ſayde God to be vniuſt, sheweth that mans pietie nor impietie neither profiteth nor diſprofiteth God: 13. and that he iudgeth al thinges rightly.
\p
\v 1 THERFORE Eliu againe ſpake theſe wordes:
\v 2 Doth thy cogitation ſeme iuſt to thee, that thou ſaydſt: \f a \fr 35.2 \ft Eliu inſiſteth much in this calumniation. for Iob neuer ſaid, that he was \+it iuſt compared with God\+it*, nor \+it iuſter then God\+it*. But that his affliction was greater then his ſinne. \+it ch. 6. v\+it*. 3. c. 23. v. 7. &c.\f* I am iuſter then God?
\v 3 For thou ſaydſt: That which is right doth not pleaſe thee: or what wil it profite thee if I ſinne?
\v 4 Therfore wil I anſwer to thy wordes, & to thy frendes with thee.
\v 5 \f b \fr 35.5 \ft Theſe are ſtrong ſentences (ſaith S. Gregorie) but they agree not (or be euil applied) to the bleſſed perſon of Iob. li. 26. c. 7.\f* Looke vp to heauen and ſee, and behold the skie, that it is higher then thou.
\v 6 If thou ſinne, what shalt thou hurt him? and if thine iniquities be multiplied, what shalt thou doe againſt him?
\v 7 Moreouer if thou doe iuſtly, what shalt thou geue him, or what shal he receiue of thy hand?
\v 8 Man that is like to thee, thy impietie shal hurt: and thy iuſtice shal helpe the ſonne of man.
\v 9 Becauſe of the multitude of calumniatours they shal cry: and shal waile for the force of the arme of tyrantes.
\v 10 And he hath not ſayd: Where is God, that made me, that hath geuen ſongs in the night?
\v 11 Who teacheth vs aboue the beaſtes of the earth, and inſtructeth vs aboue the foules of the ayre.
\v 12 There shal they crie, and he wil not heare, becauſe of the pride of the euil.
\v 13 God therfore wil not heare without cauſe, and the Omnipotent wil behold the cauſes of euerie one.
\v 14 Yea when thou shalt ſay: He conſidereth not: be iudged before him, & expect him.
\v 15 For he doth not now inferte his furie, neither doth he reuenge wickedneſſe excedingly.
\v 16 Therfore Iob in vaine openeth his mouth, and without knowledge multiplieth wordes.
\c 36
\cd Yet further Eliu sheweth, that God by his powre and wiſdome geueth to euerie one that is iuſt. 16. inferring (falſly) that al Iobs affliction is for his ſinnes. 22. diſcourſing ſtil of Gods powre, wiſdom, and prouidence.
\p
\v 1 ELIV alſo adding ſpeake theſe wordes:
\v 2 Beare with me a litle, and I wil shew to thee: for as yet I haue what to ſpeake for God.
\v 3 I wil repete my knowledge from the beginning, and I wil proue my maker iuſt.
\v 4 For in deede my wordes are \f a \fr 36.4 \ft No orator is ſo vaine but he promiſeth al truth, & ſpeaketh ſome to get credite with his auditorie.\f* without lye, and perfect knowledge shal be proued to thee.
\v 5 God doth not caſt away the mightie, wheras himſelf alſo is mightie.
\v 6 But he ſaueth not the impious, and he giueth iudgement to the poore.
\v 7 He shal not take away his eyes from the iuſt man, and he placeth kinges in the throne \f b \fr 36.7 \ft If kinges reigne wel their praiſe remaineth for euer. \+it v. 11\+it*.\f* for euer, and there they are extolled.
\v 8 And if they shal be in cheines, and be bound with the ropes of pouertie.
\v 9 He shal shew them their workes, and their wicked deedes, becauſe they haue bene violent.
\v 10 He alſo shal reuele their eare, to chaſtiſe them: and shal ſpeake, that they may returne from iniquitie:
\v 11 If they shal heare and obſerue, they shal accomplish their daies in good, and their yeares in glorie.
\v 12 But if they heare not they shal paſſe by the ſworde, and shal be conſumed in folie.
\v 13 Diſſemblers and craftie men prouoke the wrath of God, neither shal they crie when they are bound.
\v 14 Their ſoule shal dye in tempeſt, & their life among the effeminates.
\v 15 He shal deliuer the poore out of his diſtreſſe, and shal reuele his eare in tribulation.
\v 16 Therfore he shal ſaue thee moſt largely out of the narrow mouth, and not hauing foundation vnder it: and the quietneſſe of thy table shal be ful of fatneſſe.
\v 17 Thy cauſe is iudged as an impious mans, cauſe and iudgement thou shalt receiue.
\v 18 Let not therfore anger ouercome thee, that thou oppreſſe anie man: neither let multitude of gifres incline thee.
\v 19 Lay downe thy greatnes without tribulation, and al the puiſſant of ſtrength.
\v 20 \f c \fr 36.20 \ft The night is drawne long, when tentations are not ſpedely reſiſted\f* Protract not the night, that \f d \fr 36.20 \ft wherby ſtil worſe and worſe cogitations ſuccede in place of the firſt. S. Greg. li. 26. c. 38.\f* peoples may come vp for them.
\v 21 Beware thou decline not to iniquitie: for thou haſt begunne to folow it after miſerie.
\v 22 Behold, God is high in his ſtrength, and none is like to him \f e \fr 36.22 \ft Aboue al other Lawgeuers God is moſt able to punish tranſgreſſors, but moſt willing and moſt able to reward the obſeruers. S. Gregorie expoundeth this to be a prophecie \+it of Chriſt our ſingular lawgeuer. li. 27. c. 1\+it*.\f* among the lawgeuers.
\v 23 Who can ſearch his waies? or who can ſay to him: Thou haſt wrought iniquitie?
\v 24 Remember that thou knoweſt not his worke, wherof men haue ſong.
\v 25 Al men ſee him, euerie one beholdeth far of.
\v 26 Behold, God is great ſurmounting our knowledge: the number of his yeares is ineſtimable.
\v 27 Who taketh away the droppes of raine, and powreth out showers as it were gulfes of water.
\v 28 Which flow out from the clowdes, that couer al thinges from aboue.
\v 29 If he wil ſtretch forth clowdes as his tent.
\v 30 And lighten with his light from aboue, he shal couer alſo the endes of the ſea.
\v 31 For by theſe he iudgeth peoples, and geueth victuals to manie mortal men.
\v 32 In his handes \f f \fr 36.32 \ft Chriſt wil geue the glorious light of heauen which now is hidden to men that loſt terreſtrial paradiſe. \+it ibidé. c. 12\+it*.\f* he hideth the light, and commandeth it that it come agayne.
\v 33 He sheweth his freind therof, that it is his poſſeſſion, and that he may aſcend to it.
\c 37
\cd Eliu continueth his diſcourſe, shewing Gods wiſdom, powre, and iuſtice, by his meruelous workes of Meteors, 14. and vſe therof to mans commoditie. 18. which the wiſeſt men ſufficiently vnderſtand not, much leſſe may preſume (as he vniuſtly chargeth Iob) to contend with God.
\p
\v 1 VPON \f a \fr 37.1 \ft Conſideration of heauenly rewardes mentioned in the end of the former chapter, comforteth the afflicted: but thunder and other meteors being figures of Gods iudgement, ſtrike the hart with terrour.\f* this my hart is ſore afrayd, and is moued out of his place.
\v 2 Heare ye his ſpeach in the terrour of his voice, and the ſound proceding out of his mouth.
\v 3 Vnder al the heauens he conſidereth, and his light is vpon the endes of the earth.
\v 4 After him shal ſounding roare, he shal thunder with the voice of his greatnes, & shal not be found out when his voice shal be heard.
\v 5 God shal thunder in his voice meruelouſly, he that doeth great & vnſearcheable thinges.
\v 6 He that commandeth the ſnow to deſcend vpon the earth, and the winter raines, and the shower of his ſtrength.
\v 7 He that ſigneth in the hand of al men, that euerie one may know his workes.
\v 8 The beaſt shal enter into his couert, and shal abide in his denne.
\v 9 From the inner partes shal tempeſt come forth, and cold from \f b \fr 37.9 \ft North wind. or north pole.\f* Arcturus.
\v 10 When God bloweth froſt congeleth, and againe waters are powred moſt largely.
\v 11 Corne deſireth clowdes, and the clowdes ſpred their light
\v 12 Which goe round about, whither ſoeuer the wil of \f c \fr 37.12 \ft God directeth the cloudes in the ayre as a maiſter mariner gouerneth a shippe.\f* the gouerner shal lead them, to al that he shal cōmand them vpon the face of the whole earth
\v 13 Whether in one tribe, or in his land, or in what place ſo euer of his mercy he shal command them to be found
\v 14 Harken to theſe things Iob: ſtand, and conſider the maruels of God.
\v 15 Doeſt thou know when God commanded the raines, that they shew the light of his clowdes?
\v 16 Knoweſt thou the great pathes of the clowdes, and the perfect knowledges?
\v 17 Are not thy garments hote, when the earth shal be blowen with the South winde?
\v 18 Thou perhaps madſt the heauens with him, which are moſt ſound, caſt as it were of braſſe.
\v 19 Shew vs what we may ſay to him: for we are wrapped in darkenes.
\v 20 Who shal tel him the things that I ſpeake? yea if man shal ſpeake, he shal be deuoured.
\v 21 But now they ſee not the light: ſodenly the ayre shal be thickned into clowdes, and the wind paſſing by shal driue them away.
\v 22 From the North gold cometh, & toward God \f d \fr 37.22 \ft Man not able to praiſe God ſufficiently, prayſeth him with feare.\f* fearful prayſing.
\v 23 We can not find him worthely: great of ſtrength, and iudgement, and iuſtice, and he can not be vttered.
\v 24 Therfore shal men feare him, and al that ſeme to themſelues to be wiſe, shal not dare to behold him. \f 1 \fr 37.24 \ft Iob conuinced the former three with ſound anſwers this laſt and moſt arrogant with ſilence.\f*
\c 38
\cd God after terrour of a whirlewind, by way of examining his client Iob of diuers creatures about their nature, sheweth that no man hath perfect knowlege of them, much leſſe of Gods immenſitie.
\p
\v 1 BVT \f 1 \fr 38.1 \ft The third part. The tenth and laſt diſpute. God diſcuſſeth the controverſy, and giveth ſentence for Job.\f* our Lord anſwering Iob out of a whirlewind, ſayd:
\v 2 Who is this that wrappeth in ſentences with vnskilful wordes?
\v 3 Gird thy loynes as a man: I wil aske thee, and \f a \fr 38.3 \ft Onlie the Creator hath abſolute and perfect knowlege of al creatures. As may appeare by induction, or example:\f* anſwer thou me.
\v 4 Where waſt thou when I layd \f b \fr 38.4 \ft Of the creation of the earth.\f* the foundations of the earth? tel me if thou haſt vnderſtanding.
\v 5 Who ſet the meaſures therof, if thou know? or who ſtretched out the line vpon it?
\v 6 Vpon what are the foundations therof grounded? or who let downe the corner ſtone therof,
\v 7 when the morning ſtarres praiſed me together, and al the ſonnes of God made iubilation?
\v 8 Who shut in the \f c \fr 38.8 \ft Of the ſea.\f* ſea with doores when it brake forth proceding as it were out of a matrice:
\v 9 When I made a clowde the garment therof, and wrapped it in darkenes as in cloutes of infancie.
\v 10 I compaſſed it with my boundes, and put barre and doores.
\v 11 And I ſayd: Hitherto thou shalt come, and shalt not procede farder, & here thou shalt breake thy ſwelling waues.
\v 12 Didſt thou after thy birth command \f d \fr 38.12 \ft Diſtinction of lightes.\f* the morning, and shew the dawning his place.
\v 13 And didſt thou hold the extremities of the earth shaking them, and haſt thou shaken the impious out of it?
\v 14 The ſeale shal be reſtored as clay, and shal ſtand as a garment:
\v 15 From the impious their light shal be taken away, and the high arme shal be broken.
\v 16 Haſt thou entered into \f e \fr 38.16 \ft The depth of the ſea.\f* the depthes of the ſea, and walked in the loweſt partes of the great depth?
\v 17 Haue the gates of death bene open to thee, and haſt thou ſene the darkeſome doores?
\v 18 Haſt thou conſidered the bredth of the earth? tel, me if thou know al things,
\v 19 In what way the light dwelleth, and what is the place of darkeneſſe.
\v 20 That thou canſt bring euerie thing to his borders, and vnderſtand the pathes of the houſe therof.
\v 21 \f f \fr 38.21 \ft Mans owne natiuitie, and what shal happen after his death\f* Didſt thou know then that thou shouldeſt be borne? and didſt thou know the number of thy dayes?
\v 22 Haſt thou entered into the treaſures of the ſnow, or haſt thou beheld treaſures of haile?
\v 23 Which I haue prepared for the time of the enemie, for the day of fight and battel?
\v 24 What way is the \f g \fr 38.24 \ft The ſunnes light and heate.\f* light ſpred, is heate diuided vpon the earth?
\v 25 Who \f h \fr 38.25 \ft Diuers Meteors.\f* gaue courſe to the moſt vehement shower, and the way of the ſounding thunder:
\v 26 That it should rayne vpon the earth, without man in the deſert, where no mortal man abideth:
\v 27 That it should fil the deſert & deſolate ground, and should bring forth grene graſſe?
\v 28 Who is the father of rayne? or who begot the droppes of dewe?
\v 29 Out of whoſe wombe came forth yſe? & froſt from heauen who ingendred?
\v 30 Waters are hardened like ſtone, and the face of the depth is congealed?
\v 31 Shalt thou be able to ioyne together the shining \f i \fr 38.31 \ft Starres, and planetes.\f* ſtarres Pleiades, or canſt thou diſſipate the circuite of Acturus?
\v 32 Doeſt thou bring forth the day ſtarre in his time, and make Euening ſtarre to riſe vpon the children of the earth?
\v 33 Doeſt thou know the order of heauen, and shalt thou Put downe the reaſon therof on the earth?
\v 34 Shalt thou eleuate the voice in the clowde, & the violence of the waters couer thee?
\v 35 Shalt thou ſend lightenings, and wil they goe, and returning shal they ſay to thee: Here we are?
\v 36 Who put \f k \fr 38.36 \ft Man hath his knowlege from God.\f* wiſedom in the hart of man? or who gaue the \f l \fr 38.36 \ft And the cock hath skil, which man wanteth.\f* cocke vnderſtanding?
\v 37 Who shal declare the maner of the heauens, and the harmonie of heauen who shal make to \f 1 \fr 38.37 \ft \+it or reſt from motion\+it*.\f* ſleepe.
\v 38 When was the duſt powred on the earth, and the cloddes compact together?
\v 39 \f m \fr 38.39 \ft In ſome Editions the 39. chap. begineth here. shewing by induction of ſenſible thinges (as before of inſenſible) that onlie God knoweth the nature of al creatures.\f* Shalt thou take a praye for the liones and fil the appetite of her whelpes.
\v 40 When they lie in the dennes, and in holes ſit in wayte?
\v 41 Who prepareth for the rauen her meate, when her yong ones crie to God, wandring about, becauſe they haue not meate?
\c 39
\cd God Almightie proſecuteth his diſcourſe, shewing his admirable powre and prouidence in liuing creatures. 34. Wherupon Iob acknowlegeth his owne ouerſight in ſome light wordes.
\p
\v 1 HAST \f a \fr 39.1 \ft By Gods meruelous prouidence appearing in the natural inſtinct of other creatures man may conſider that the ſame is greater towards him. And therfore God here propoſeth the examples of.\f* thou knowen the time when the \f b \fr 39.1 \ft VVild goates.\f* wild goates bring forth yong among the rockes, or haſt thou obſerued the \f c \fr 39.1 \ft Hyndes.\f* hyndes when they fawne?
\v 2 Haſt thou numbred the monethes of their conceiuing, and knowen the time of their bearing?
\v 3 They bowe downe themſelues to bring forth yong, and they caſt them, and make roarings.
\v 4 Their yong are ſeperated, and goe to feed: they goe forth, and returne not to them.
\v 5 Who hath diſmiſt the \f d \fr 39.5 \ft VVild aſſes.\f* wilde aſſe free, and who hath looſed his bondes?
\v 6 To whom I haue geuen a houſe in the wildernes, and his tabernacles in the land of ſaltneſſe.
\v 7 He contemneth the multitude of the citie, the crie of the exactour he heareth not.
\v 8 He looketh about the mountaines of his paſture, and ſeeketh out al greene places.
\v 9 Wil the \f e \fr 39.9 \ft Vnicornes.\f* Rhinoceres ſerue thee, and wil he tarie at thy ſtal?
\v 10 Shal thou tie the Rhinoceres with thy coller to plough, or wil he breake the cloddes of the valleys after thee?
\v 11 Shalt thou haue confidence in his great ſtrength, and leaue thy labours vnto him?
\v 12 Wilt thou credit him that he wil render thee the ſeede, and gather together thy barne floore?
\v 13 The wing of the \f f \fr 39.13 \ft Oſtriches,\f* Oſtrich is like to the winges of the \f g \fr 39.13 \ft Falcons, or Ierfalcous, & other haukes.\f* Herodius, and of the hawke.
\v 14 When she leaueth her egges on the earth, thou perhaps wilt heate them in the duſt.
\v 15 She forgeteth that foote may treade vpon them, or beaſt of the fild breake them.
\v 16 She is hardened toward her yong, as though they were not hers, ſhe hath laboured in vaine no feare compelling her.
\v 17 For God hath depriued her of wiſedom neither hath he geuen her vnderſtanding.
\v 18 When time shal be, she ſetteth vp the winges on high: she skorneth the horſe & his rider.
\v 19 Shalt thou geue ſtrentgh to the \f h \fr 39.19 \ft Horſes are of ſingular great corege.\f* horſe, or put neying about his necke?
\v 20 Shalt thou raiſe him vp as Locuſtes? the glorie of his noſthrels is terrour.
\v 21 He diggeth the earth with his houfe, he prawnſeth boldly, he goeth forward to meete the armed men.
\v 22 He contemneth feare, neither yealdeth he to the ſword.
\v 23 Vpon him shal the quiuer ſound, the ſpeare ſhal gliſter and the shilde.
\v 24 Feruent and foming he ſuppeth the earth, neither doth he make account when the noyſe of the trumpet ſoundeth.
\v 25 When he ſhal heare the trumpet he ſayth: Vah, he ſmelleth battel far of, the exhortation of the captaines, and the crie of the armie.
\v 26 Doth the \f i \fr 39.26 \ft Haukes wherof Ariſtotel ſaith there be ten kindes: Plinie ſixtene.\f* hawke waxe fethered by thy wiſedom, ſpreding her winges to the South?
\v 27 Shal the \f k \fr 39.27 \ft Eagles, of moſt ſtrong ſight.\f* eagle mount at thy commandment, and put her neſt in high places?
\v 28 She abideth in rockes, and tarieth among cragged flintes, and ſtonie hilles where is no acceſſe.
\v 29 Thence she beholdeth the praye, and her eies ſee a far of.
\v 30 Her yong ones shal licke bloud: & wherſoeuer the carcaſſe shal be, she is preſent by and by.
\v 31 And our Lord added, and ſpake to Iob:
\v 32 He that contendeth with God is he quieted ſo eaſily? Verely he that reproueth God, ought to anſwer him.
\v 33 But Iob anſwering our Lord, ſayd:
\v 34 I that haue ſpoken \f l \fr 39.34 \ft If we diſcuſſe al Iobs wordes \+it (ſaith S. Gregorie\+it*.) we shal find nothing wickedly ſpoken, but only ſmale ſpeece of pride in ſpeaking too much of his owne afflictió and too litle of Gods goodnes towards him \+it li.23.c.1\+it*.\f* lightly what can I anſwer? I wil put my hand vpon my mouth.
\v 35 One thing I haue ſpoken, which I would I had not ſayd: and an other, to the which thinges I wil adde no more.
\c 40
\cd Our Lord further sheweth that mans powre, nor iuſtice, is not comparable to Gods. 10. as appeareth in ouercoming Behemoth, 20. and Leuiathan.
\p
\v 1 AND our Lord anſwering Iob out of the whirlewinde, ſayd:
\v 2 Gird thy loynes as a man: I wil aske thee, and doe thou tel me.
\v 3 Shalt thou make my \f a \fr 40.3 \ft Though Iob had truly auouched that his ſinnes deſerued not ſo great afflictions, yet he ought to haue acknowleged that God afflicted him iuſtly, for ſome other cauſe knowne to God, but vnknowen to him, which he not confeſſing ſemed to make Gods iudgement vniuſt: or, \+it of none effect. S. Greg. li.32.c.3\+it*.\f* iudgement of none effect: and condemne me, that thou mayſt be iuſtified?
\v 4 And haſt thou an arme as God, and doſt thou thunder with like voice.
\v 5 Put beautie about thee, and ſet vp thy ſelfe aloft, and be glorious, and put on goodlie garmentes.
\v 6 Diſperſe the prowde in thy furie, and beholding euerie arrogant man, humble him.
\v 7 Behold al the prowde, and confound them, and deſtroy the impious in their place.
\v 8 Hide them in the duſt together, and plunge their faces in the pit.
\v 9 And I wil confeſſe, that thy right hand is able to ſaue thee.
\v 10 Behold, \f b \fr 40.10 \ft An Elephant the greateſt of al beaſtes, of long life, ſtrong, meke, temperate, chaiſt, ouercome by the Vnicorne, or Dragon, or taken by the noſe & ledde away. How much more doth Gods prouidence geue man powre to ouercome the diuel?\f* Behemoth whom I made with thee, shal eate hay as it were an oxe.
\v 11 His ſtrength is in his loynes, and his powre in the nauil of his bellie.
\v 12 He gathereth together his taile as the ceder tree, the ſinewes of his ſtones are perplexe.
\v 13 His bones are as pipes of braſſe, his griſtle as it were plates of iron.
\v 14 He is the beginning of the wayes of God, which made him, he shal applie his ſword.
\v 15 To him the mountaines beare graſſe: al the beaſtes of the filde shal play there.
\v 16 He ſleepeth vnder the shadow, in the ſecrete of the reede, and in moyſt places.
\v 17 Shadowes doe protect his shadow, the willowes of the torrent shal compaſſe him.
\v 18 Loe, he shal ſup vp the riuer, and shal not meruaile: and he hath confidence that Iordan may runne into his mouth.
\v 19 In his eies as with a hooke he shal take him, and with ſtakes he shal boare through his noſthrels.
\v 20 Canſt thou drawe out the \f c \fr 40.20 \ft An huge great fish. perhaps, the whale, exceding mans powre to be managed, yet is ſubiect (as alſo the diuel ſignified therby) to Gods powre, and prouidence.\f* Leuiathan with a hooke, and with a rope shalt thou tye his tongue?
\v 21 Shalt thou put a ring in his noſthrels, or bore through his iaw with a buckle?
\v 22 Wil he multiplie prayers to thee, or ſpeake to thee gentle wordes?
\v 23 Wil he make a couenant with thee, and shalt thou take him to be a ſeruant for euer?
\v 24 Shalt shou delude him as a birde, or tye him for thy handmaydes?
\v 25 Shal freindes cut him, merchantes diuide him?
\v 26 Shalt thou fil nettes with his skinne, and the cabbin of fishes with his heade?
\v 27 Lay thy hand vpon him: remember battel, and adde to ſpeake no more.
\v 28 Behold his hope shal fruſtrate him, and in the ſight of al he shal be caſt downe headlong.
\c 41
\cd Leuiathan is further deſcribed by the peculiar partes of his bodie. and terrible compoſition of al his members.
\p
\v 1 NOT as \f a \fr 41.1 \ft God ruleth al his creatures, not with crueltie as a tyranne, but with iuſtice, eaſe, and powre.\f* cruel wil I raiſe him: for who can reſiſt my countenance?
\v 2 Who hath geuen me befote, that I may render vnto him? Al thinges that are vnder heauen be myne.
\v 3 I wil not ſpare him, and his mightie wotdes, and framed to beſeech.
\v 4 Who shal reuele the face of his garment: and who shal enter into the middes of his mouth?
\v 5 Who shal open the gate of his countenance? dreade is round about his teeth.
\v 6 His bodie as shildes that are caſt, compact with skales faſt cleauing together.
\v 7 One is ioyned to an other, and not ſo much as anie ayre entereth betwen them:
\v 8 One shal ſticke to an other, & holding eche other, they shal not be ſeperated.
\v 9 His ſneeſing is as the shining of fire, & his eies as the twinklings of the morning.
\v 10 Out of his mouth procede lampes, as it were torches of lighted fire.
\v 11 Out of his noſthrels procedeth ſmoke, as it were of a pot heated and boyling.
\v 12 His breath maketh coales to burne, & a flame cometh fotth out of his mouth.
\v 13 In his necke shal ſtrength abide, & needines goeth befote his face.
\v 14 The members of his flesh cleaue together one to an other: \f b \fr 41.14 \ft God at laſt deſtroyeth him whom man can not ouercome.\f* he shal ſend lightnings againſt him, and he shal not be caried to an other place.
\v 15 His hart shal be hardened as a ſtone, and shal be ſtifly compact as the ſmithes ſtithie.
\v 16 When he shal be taken away, the \f c \fr 41.16 \ft Angels with reuerent feare doe honour Gods powre. And valient mariners and other ſoldiars are terrified when they ſee this ſo huge a fish. Myſtically, Gods preachers and perfecteſt ſeruantes ſhal naturally feare the terrour of Gods iudgement.\f* Angels shal feare, and being feared shal be purged.
\v 17 When the ſword shal apprehend him, neither ſpeare, nor breſtplate shal be able to abide.
\v 18 For he shal eſteme yron as chaffe, and braſſe, as rotten wood.
\v 19 The bowman shal not put him to flight, the ſtones of the ſling, to him are turned into ſtubble.
\v 20 As ſtubble wil he eſteme the hammer, and he wil laugh him to skorne that shaketh the ſpeare.
\v 21 The beames of the ſunne shal be vnder him, and he shal ſtraw gold vnder him as durt.
\v 22 He shal make the deepe ſea to boyle as a pot, and shal put it as when ointmentes boyle.
\v 23 A path shal shine after him, he shal eſteme the depth as waxing old.
\v 24 There is no power vpon the earth, that may be compared with him, who is made to feare no man.
\v 25 He ſeeth euerie high thing, he is \f d \fr 41.25 \ft And the diuel reigneth ouer proude men. \+it S. Greg. li.34.c 4.& 17\+it*.\f* king ouer al the children of pryde.
\c 42
\cd Holie Iob inſtructed & comforted by Gods diſcourſe, acknowlegeth his fault, and craueth pardon for his ouerſight in ſpeach, or cogitation. 7. God iuſtifieth his cauſe againſt his freindes. 9. They offer ſacrifice for their offence. 10. Al thinges proſper with Iob, duble to that he had before. 16. and he dieth happely.
\p
\v 1 AND Iob anſwering our Lord, ſayd:
\v 2 I know that thou canſt doe al thinges, and no cogitation is hid from thee.
\v 3 Who is this, that concealeth counſel without knowledge? Therfore haue I ſpoken \f a \fr 42.3 \ft Iob here ſimply ackowledged his error in ſpeaking ſo much in defence of his owne innocencie, and ſo litle of Gods prouidence in afflicting him, for his more merite, and Gods more glorie.\f* vnwiſely, and the thinges that did excede my knowledge beyond meſure.
\v 4 Heare (I beſech thee) and I wil ſpeake: I wil aske thee, and do thou tel me.
\v 5 With the hearing of the eare I haue heard thee, but now my eye ſeeth thee.
\v 6 Therfore \f b \fr 42.6 \ft Before he defended a truth againſt his oppoſite freindes, now with more reſignation he contented himſelf with his affliction.\f* I reprehend my ſelf, and I do penance in imbers and ashes.
\v 7 And after our Lord ſpake theſe wordes to Iob, he ſayd to Eliphaz the Themanite: My furie is wrath againſt thee, and againſt thy two frendes, becauſe you haue not ſpoken right before me, as my ſeruant Iob.
\v 8 Take therfore vnto you ſeuen oxen, and ſeuen rammes, and goe to my ſeruant Iob, and offer holocauſte for your ſelues: and my ſeruant Iob shal pray for you: his face I wil receiue, that the follie be not imputed to you: for you haue not ſpoken right thinges before me, as my ſeruant Iob.
\v 9 Eliphaz therfore the Themanite, and Baldad the Suhite, and Sophar the Naamathite went, and did as our Lord had ſpoken to them, and our Lord receiued the face of Iob.
\v 10 Our Lord alſo was turned at the \f c \fr 42.10 \ft Iob did penance both for himſelf and others.\f* penance of Iob, when he prayed for his freindes. And our Lord added al thinges whatſoeuer had bene Iobes, duble.
\v 11 And al his brethren came to him, and al his ſiſters, and al that knew him befote, and they did eate bread with him in his houſe: and wagged the head vpon him, and comforted him vpon al the euil that God had brought in vpon him. And euerie man gaue him one ewe, and one earelet of gold.
\v 12 And our Lord bleſſed the laſt daies of Iob more then his beginning. And he had fourtene thouſand sheepe, and ſix thouſand camels, & a thouſand yoke of oxen, and a thouſand she aſſes.
\v 13 And he had \f d \fr 42.13 \ft In that he had al other thinges duble, and children in the ſame number as before, it is a ſigne that the former perished not but died in good ſtate:\f* ſeuen ſonnes, and three daughters.
\v 14 And he called the name of one Dies, and the name of the ſecond Caſſia, & the name of the third Cornuſtibij.
\v 15 And there were not found in al the earth wemen ſo beautiful as the daughters of Iob: and their father gaue them inheritance among their brethren.
\v 16 And Iob liued after theſe thinges, an hundred fourtie yeares, and he ſaw his children, and his childrens children, vnto the fourth generation, and he died an old man, and ful of daies.
\s1 A BRIEF RECAPITULATION OF THIS STRANGE AND SACRED HISTORY.
\ip For avoiding prolixity (this volume growing great) we have for the most part contracted our \f - \ft Why we have made few annotations in this book.\f* Annotations into the margent, making very few others in this book, which otherwise offered much more occasion, as well for explicating hard places, as of other doctrinal and moral instructions. Neither indeed can ordinary Annotations well suffice for understanding of this, and other hard books. But rather large Commentaries are required, such as \it S. Gregory\it*, and other ancient Fathers, as also \it F. John de Pineda\it*, and others have lately written; whereto we therefore remit the learned readers. And for the benefit of others of our nation, we shall here briefly recapitulate the sum, and principal points of this holy and admirable history, consisting in a singular holy man's conflicts and glorious victory, against invisible and visible adversaries, both in prosperous and adverse fortune. \f - \ft The argument of this history.\f*
\ip First, this holy man \it Job in all aboundance of wealth\it* and riches, \it blessed with\it* many \it children\it* (ch. \f - \ft Job in prosperity was tempted invisibly more than ordinary men of lower state, or less perfection.\f* 1.) sitting in a princely throne and \it royal dignity (ch. 29.) in the land of Hus\it*, was not only assaulted with common tentations of the envious enemy, as all are that live piously in God, but so \it much\it* \f - \ft \+it 2. Tim. 3.\+it*\f* \it the more\it*, as he was more godly, sincerer, and perfecter than \it other men\it*: yet he \it never set his heart upon worldly\it* or temporal \it things\it*, but with all due fear so \it served God, and parted from evil\it*, that \it Satan\it* himself (the calumniator of mankind) could not charge him with any sin at all. Though he would not for all that confess him to be just or perfect: but for further trial of him, demanded and obtained licence of God to touch all his possessions, and so \it bereaved him of all his goods and children\it* in one day. And when he \it persevering\it* constant \it in virtue, thanking God for all, not\it* \f - \ft Much more by loss of all his goods & children in one day. Most of by bodily affliction. And reviling of his wife.\f* \it sinning in his lips, neither speaking any foolish thing against God\it*, the devil getting more ample permission to touch his bones and flesh (chap. 2.) \it suddenly struck him with a most grievous botch\it* (or boyle) \it from the sole of the foot to the top of the head: who sitting on a dunghill, and scraping the corruption of his sores with a shell\it* in extreme pain, \it his\it* own \it wife\it*, by the devil's suggestion, \it reviled him\it* for his sincerity, \it and provoked him to blaspheme God\it*: but he severely reprehended her folly, still keeping necessary patience.
\ip Then came \it three\it* special \it friends\it*, noble wise men (or little Kings) to visit, and comfort him, who \it in seven days not speaking one word of consolation\it*, nor entering into any discourse with him, at last \it Job\it* himself (chap. 3.) broke this long silence (but not his patience) \it lamentably bewailing the extremity of his pains\it*, imputing all to the miseries of man's estate, corrupted by sin, \it discoursed\it* \f - \ft Holy Job lamented his affliction, & the general miseries of man.\f* \it of certain penal evils\it*, or maladies ensuing thereupon, \it wishing\it* for his own part (if it had so been God's pleasure, for he sincerely feared God) \it that either he had not been born, or been shortly taken out of this world, cursing sin and the proper effects thereof\it* remaining in man, \it wishing\it* also \it to have wanted the ordinary benefits of\it* education in his infancy, and all his former prosperity, so that he might have escaped the calamities, wherewith he was now afflicted. All which he uttering in way of contemning all worldly things, and supposing his friends there present would have so understood him, and had compassion with him: they contrarywise (by art of the devil, God so \f - \ft Where Job expected comfort in tribulation, the devil procured him more affliction.\f* permitting) \it fell into indignation, and\it* in stead of comforting their most afflicted friend, \it sharply reprehended him\it*, rashly judged his conscience, and falsely \it condemned him\it*, not only \it of impatient speech\it*, as offensive to God, and his Angels, and to all good men, but also of other enormous sins: as \it pride, tyranny, presumption, hypocrisy, and blasphemy\it*, because heretofore he seemed to the world as just and holy, and now (as they imagined) in his deserved punishment, charged God with injustice. Whereupon grew diverse long disputes between Job and his three friends, a fourth also intruding himself, when the others ceased. \it So that Job endured nine conflicts\it*, and \it in the tenth\it* \f - \ft Job sore afflicted in body had nine several conflicts about the cause thereof, before it was decided.\f* \it God judged him the victor\it*, which shall yet better appear, if we repeat the sum of their arguments, and his answers, with God's decision of the controversy.
\ip In the first conflict \it Eliphaz\it* the chiefest of Job's friends (in the 4. and 6. chapters) \it accused Job of\it* \f - \ft The first conflict.\f* \it great impatience, and insolency\it* against God, also both \it him and his family of tyranny, like to a cruel lion, and lion's whelps\it*, alleging for proof the prosperity of good men, punishment of the wicked, and a particular vision. Adjured him therefore to acknowledge and repent the same. But \it Job\it* (in other two chapters) \it avouched that indeed his afflictions were greater than his sins deserved\it*, \f - \ft The many point of the controversy.\f* relying upon his innocency known to his own conscience. Described also the manifold calamities of man's life, desired to die, and so to end his worldly miseries.
\ip Then \it Baldad\it* the second opposite friend (in the 8. chap.) \it pretending to free God's judgement\it* \f - \ft The second conflict.\f* from all shew or resemblance of injustice, \it charged Job and his children with former wickedness\it*, and him as injurious to God in his speeches, of which if he would repent, he should be healed, and prosper as before: \it Arguing\it* in general, \it that God never afflicteth the innocent, nor assisteth the\it* \f - \ft The ground of these men's error. of our days, that rely each one upon his own private spirit.\f* \it malignant\it*. Insinuating thereby, that Job was an hypocrite. Whereto Job answered (ch. 9. & 10.) that indeed \it no man may compare, nor justify himself before God\it*. Nevertheless it standeth well with God's justice, power, & wisdom, that innocents be sometimes exercised with tribulations, more than their offences deserve.
\ip Thirdly \it Sophar\it* (the third disputer) \it assaulted Job\it* (ch. 11.) \it imputing his speech and defence of\it* \f - \ft The third conflict.\f* \it himself to loquacity, and audacious temerity, in\it* that in that he desired to know the causes of God's providence, in so grievous afflicting him. Of which faults holy \it Job purged himself\it* (in the three next chapters) still maintaining his innocency, according to his own conscience better known to himself than to them, desiring God to instruct him, if he had any unknown sins. Discoursed also much more profoundly of God's power, wisdom, justice, and providence, as well in general, as towards himself in particular: and professed his faith, and great confidence of the Resurrection. Again \f - \ft The fourth conflict.\f* \it Eliphaz\it* (ch. 15.) \it more bitterly\it* than before \it condemned Job of presumption, and blasphemy\it*, discoursed of man's corruptness and proneness to sin, describing the manners of hypocrites, and other impious men, with their miserable ends, and argued \it Job\it* for such a one. Who (in the next two chapters) \it expostulated with these his friends\it*, that they coming with pretence to comfort him, did so violently afflict him, by charging him with false and heinous crimes, his own conscience better knowing and testifying his former life, and state of his soul, than that their imaginations could alter his judgement. And so \it with contempt of this world\it*, & desire of death and rest, \it appealed to God's judgement against his three friends\it*, touching the matter in controversy. In the mean time comforted himself with meditation of the next world.
\ip \it Baldad\it* likewise \it replied\it* (ch. 18.) \it with hot contention\it*, accusing job of insolent impatience, \f - \ft The fifth conflict.\f* inculcating the grievous punishments both of him, and others for their impiety. In answer whereto \it he lamented again the want of expected comfort\it*, especially by such friends. Still comforted himself with assured faith of the Resurrection.
\ip \it Sophar\it* also (ch. 20.) \it attempted again to convince Job of impiety and hypocrisy\it*, by the miserable \f - \ft The sixth conflict.\f* and speedy fall of wicked men after prosperity: for so he imagined Job to be fallen into irrecoverable misery. But \it Job shewed\it* the contrary, \it that some wicked men prosper long\it*, yea all their life, and the same long, and then \it in a moment go down to hell\it*, and so the argument of present affliction proved not their opinion against him.
\ip \it Eliphaz disputed\it* the third time (ch. 22.) \it contending that the causes of affliction are not to be\it* \f - \ft The seventh conflict.\f* \it attributed to God's secret providence\it*, but to assured sins of the wicked. Upon whom only he supposed, that afflictions fall: inferring that Job was guilty of enormous crimes, and gross errors, Urged him therefore to return to God, that he might be restored to former prosperity. \it Job again appealed to God's sentence\it*, not in his terror, nor rigour of his justice, but against his adversaries in this quarrel, \it describing God's power, and wisdom, by which he permitteth the innocent to be afflicted, and the wicked to prosper\it*: no man knowing how soon or how late all shall receive as they deserve.
\ip Moreover \it Baldad disputed\it* the third time, very briefly (ch. 25.) \it endeavouring to terrify Job from\it* \f - \ft The eighth conflict.\f* \it further answering\it*, and especially \it from appealing to God's judgement\it*. But \it Job\it* largely (in six ensuing chapters) \it discoursed divinely of God's sovereign Majesty, Power, Wisdom, exact justice, and infinite Mercy\it*. Also of wicked men's destruction, \it of his own former prosperity, and present calamity\it*, together \it with his good works\it*, and innocency, which he still avouched in respect of great iniquities.
\ip After that Job and his three friends ceased, nothing being agreed upon in the point of \f - \ft The ninth conflict.\f* controversy, the devil yet ceased not, but stirred up a young man, called \it Eliu, proud and arrogant\it*, but not unlearned, who \it abruptly condemned them all\it*, to wit, Job of pertinacy, the others of insufficiency. And therefore took upon him to convince Job, though the others could not. Very \f - \ft Newest Sectaries hold themselves the wisest.\f* like to late-rising \it Protestants or Puritans, bragging that by new arguments\it*, and proofs never heard of, \it they will overthrow the Papists\it*, or Catholic Roman Church, and doctrine, which all former enemies, \it Jews, Pagans, Turks, and Heretics, nor Hell gates, could not overcome\it*. This \it young Eliu\it* therefore, \it with his Private spirit\it*, wiser in his own conceipt than all that went before him, \f - \ft Especially these\f* \it assaulted\it* constant \it Job\it* (ch. 32. and five more ensuing) with many words, and brags, often challenging and provoking, but not extorting any answer from so grave a man to his frivolous and idle arguments, largely discoursing of things either not denied, or so manifest false, that every mean servant of God, could easily convince them, and never approaching to the main controversy, only railed against holy Job, \it charging him more\it* furiously than any had done before, \it with impiety, impatience, ignorance, pride, blasphemy\it*, and obstinacy, vices far from Job's sanctity, dilating also of God's justice, mercy, wisdom, power, and providence, and that no man ought to contend, nor expostulate with God, that afflictions must be borne patiently, and that God is just, and marvelous in his works: whereof no wiseman ever doubted, and so Job convinced him with silence.
\ip But \it God himself\it* for decision of all (from ch. 38. to the end of the Book) first by way of \f - \ft In the tenth place God decided the controversy.\f* examining \it instructed Job\it* more particularly, reciting many marvelous works of nature, shewing thereby his Divine Majesty, Power, and Wisdom, \it exercising Job in more patience, and\it* withal \it perfecting him in humility\it*. So that with all reverent fear and subjection, he offered and submitted himself to God's only good pleasure. Then finally \it God gave sentence that Job had defended the truth, & his three friends had erred\it*. Whom after Sacrifice, and Job's prayer for them, he pardoned, \f - \ft Penitents pardoned.\f* restored Job to health, and to double prosperity, of all he had lost before, giving him also long \f - \ft Job rewarded.\f* life, and a happy end.
\ip In this history besides the literal sense, shewing that Job was just and sincere, and not for his \f - \ft The literal sense of this history.\f* sins (as his friends falsely supposed) but for his more merit was most extremely afflicted, and afterwards restored to health and wealth: we have also here in the Allegorical sense, \it an\it* \f - \ft Allegorical.\f* \it especial figure of Christ\it*. Who as he was absolutely most innocent, and most perfect: so was he without comparison most afflicted of all mankind. Likewise Job's restoration to better state than before, signified in the Anagogical sense, \it the Resurrection\it*, and restoration of better, and most \f - \ft Anagogical.\f* glorious qualities in the blessed, \it with fullness of days, in eternal glory\it*. Finally in the Moral sense (which S. Gregory most especially prosecuteth) all Christians have here a \it most notable\it* \f - \ft Moral.\f* \it example of\it* all virtues, namely \it in patience\it*, wherein Job proceeded by degrees to great perfection. For he was first tried by the loss of all his goods and children, and was proved to be very \f - \ft Holy Job proceeded by degrees to perfect patience.\f* patient. He was then most grievously tormented in body, & being left without comfort, albeit he lamentably bewailed so great extremity, wishing such days had been prevented: yet he neither spoke against God, nor good man, nor his own soul, and according to truth avouched & defended his own innocency. And at last by God's inspiration, and sweet consolation, he reprehended himself of former imperfections uttered in some words, and with full resignation to God's will, sustained all his losses and pains, not only with contentment, but also with joy.
